- Hello, and welcome
to another very special

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.650
episode of Trash Taste.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.840
We are joined by another guest today.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.220
I'm sorry, I completely forgot,

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.750
it's been a while since I've hosted.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.580
I'm your host for today, Garnt,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.770
and with me are as usual, the boys.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:53.603
- What up?

00:01:53.603 --> 00:01:55.500
- And today we have a very special guest.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.870
Now, in case there is
one person in YouTube

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.390
who doesn't know who you are,

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.030
do you want to introduce
yourself and what you do?

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.013
- Oh my God, that's a lot of pressure.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.440
My name is Anthony Padilla.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.570
Some people say Padilla,
I'm totally fine with that,

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.701
but I do like to say
Padilla like tortilla.

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:14.100
(Garnt laughing)

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.960
That's how I get people to memorize it.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.400
Let's see, so,

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.350
God, how deep do we want to
go into this history here?

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.450
- You're a YouTube legend!
(Anthony and Garnt laughing)

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.220
Just admit it, Anthony!

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.690
- We have two-plus hours.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:25.523
- We have two-plus hours-

00:02:25.523 --> 00:02:26.951
- Are we doing two-plus hours?

00:02:26.951 --> 00:02:27.784
- Yeah, let's do it.

00:02:27.784 --> 00:02:29.010
- Okay, let's get deep, guys.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.970
So I started with my friend Ian.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.834
I started a YouTube channel
called Smosh in 2005,

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:37.421
- Never heard of it.
- What's Smosh?

00:02:37.421 --> 00:02:40.033
- 17 years, half of my age ago.

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:41.460
- Oh my God.
- Jesus.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.185
- Half of my age ago.
- Oh my God.

00:02:43.185 --> 00:02:44.018
- Half your life has been on YouTube.

00:02:44.018 --> 00:02:45.180
- Half of my life, yes.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.480
Oh shit, I'm having to
rethink so many things.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.433
I'm gonna need that 2 1/2 hours.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.530
And then in 2017, I kind of
split off to do my own thing.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.960
Been doing an independent channel

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.190
which has slowly evolved

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.550
into me kind of doing deep dive interviews

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:05.790
with either groups of people or one person

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.790
that has some kind of
popularity surrounding them.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:16.610
And we drill in deep into the
psychology behind blowing up

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.973
or if it's a group of people,

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.440
I like to kind of get in
there and understand them.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.720
I feel like so many people
have preconceived ideas

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.020
about what a group or what
a label or identity means,

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.300
so I like to kind of show
that there is variation

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.520
and nuance from individual to individual.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:36.780
- Yeah, they're really good.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:37.613
- Thank you.

00:03:37.613 --> 00:03:38.550
- I mean, this is pretty intimidating,

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.520
'cause not only are you
like, the OG of YouTube,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.725
I can say that, you are like-

00:03:42.725 --> 00:03:45.901
- You are, it's not
even a subjective thing,

00:03:45.901 --> 00:03:47.724
it's like, objectively you are an OG.

00:03:47.724 --> 00:03:49.170
- You are the OG of YouTube,

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:53.396
but also you are now the
interviewer of YouTubers

00:03:53.396 --> 00:03:54.229
and now we're interviewing you.

00:03:54.229 --> 00:03:56.351
- We're gonna reverse
engineer this interview.

00:03:56.351 --> 00:03:58.440
(all laughing)

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.640
- My stuff is so different,

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.473
when I came here, I was like,

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:01.440
"So what are we talking about today?"

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.106
You're like-
- I dunno.

00:04:03.106 --> 00:04:04.800
(all laughing)

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.633
- We'll figure it out!

00:04:05.633 --> 00:04:06.466
- When I go into mine I'm like,

00:04:06.466 --> 00:04:08.490
"Okay, okay, I've got like
this 45 bullet points,

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:09.840
I know where to get through these."

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.330
I over-plan so much.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.040
- That shows, that shows though,

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.051
it's good, it reflects in the qualities.

00:04:15.051 --> 00:04:15.990
- [Garnt] Yeah, yeah,
it really, really shows.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:17.580
- My favorite stuff is always the stuff

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.830
that comes out of stuff
that happens in the moment

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:20.820
that was never planned,

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:21.793
and that is your whole show!
- Yes!

00:04:21.793 --> 00:04:22.626
That's Trash Taste, baby!

00:04:22.626 --> 00:04:24.090
- That's your whole show!
- That's Trash Taste, baby!

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.293
- Yeah, on YouTube it'll
say top moment, all of it.

00:04:26.293 --> 00:04:27.672
- All of it.
- All of it.

00:04:27.672 --> 00:04:28.987
- 'Cause it kinda hit me when
you came in, you were like,

00:04:28.987 --> 00:04:30.027
"So what are we talking
about today, guys?"

00:04:30.027 --> 00:04:32.070
And we just look at each
other and we were just like,

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:32.903
- What do you mean?

00:04:32.903 --> 00:04:34.920
- "Uh, shit, we're
talking to Anthony today,

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.000
fuck, he's the interviewer

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.640
and we don't have any
interview questions."

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.890
- I was like, "Do they have
some kind of secret thing

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:41.760
that they're not telling me,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.793
some kind of prank that they're gonna do?"

00:04:43.793 --> 00:04:45.630
They're like, "Should we tell him?

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:46.463
No."

00:04:46.463 --> 00:04:48.930
- We're gonna hit you with a
low blow in about 30 minutes.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.210
- Just grill me.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:51.180
- So actually, I've always wondered,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.460
how much prep do you
do for your interviews?

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.593
'Cause as we've just
established, we do zero,

00:04:55.593 --> 00:04:57.103
zero prep here.
(Anthony laughing)

00:04:57.103 --> 00:05:00.660
- The only prepping we do is,
who are we getting on today?

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:01.950
- Yeah, let's see.

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:02.783
So the show starts,

00:05:02.783 --> 00:05:05.310
I call it I Spent A Day
With, whatever the topic

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.690
or whatever the person's name is.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:08.523
And at first it was like four people

00:05:08.523 --> 00:05:09.356
that I would sit down with

00:05:09.356 --> 00:05:12.090
and it would amount to like, eight hours.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.390
Now it's a little bit shorter,
like two to three hours,

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.490
if it's just one guest,
a little bit longer,

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:20.400
but I do a pre-interview call

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.410
which is like an hour,
hour and a half or two,

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.233
and then I work backwards
to try to figure out

00:05:25.233 --> 00:05:27.120
what questions I'm gonna come up with

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.500
based on the things that we talked about.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.275
So, God, I cannot even quantify.

00:05:31.275 --> 00:05:32.108
- It's a lot of prep.

00:05:32.108 --> 00:05:33.900
- What made you start
doing pre-interviews?

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:34.770
is it 'cause you maybe had

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:35.603
some guests somewhere you're like,

00:05:35.603 --> 00:05:37.410
"Fuck it, I could have
done way better job"?

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:38.243
- A little bit of that.

00:05:38.243 --> 00:05:43.050
It was also bringing on guests
that have really big stories

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.367
and I felt like I couldn't
just go in there and be like,

00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:47.550
"So, tell me about your childhood?"

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.733
And they just go and I'm like,

00:05:49.733 --> 00:05:51.693
I think they probably got everything.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.240
Also I realized that in the interviews

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.280
I was cutting out a lot of stuff.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.330
I was like, it's a lot of me

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:58.980
trying to figure out where this is going.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.290
I kind of wanna have an idea
of where we're going first.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.570
And I realized that I was like,

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.690
every night before the
next day was an interview,

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.390
I would be up until 4:00
AM, like 12 hours straight,

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:10.680
like shit, I don't know
what I'm talking about!

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:11.820
I gotta learn every single thing

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.760
about this person's life, drill down.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:15.810
I interviewed VTubers,

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.910
that's actually how I found
out about this podcast.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.460
I saw you had interviewed
a VTuber as well.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:21.430
- Oh yeah, yeah, yeah, Calli, yeah.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:23.460
- Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.590
And I watched that episode

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.237
and I was learning
everything about VTubers

00:06:27.237 --> 00:06:30.450
and I was like, wait, I
could just talk to them,

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.700
have them tell me the important things,

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.440
and then I could reverse
engineer the questions

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.860
based on the conversations
that they want to have.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.190
- So basically you take out
some of the research time

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:42.570
where you don't even
know if you're going down

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:43.917
the wrong rabbit hole.
- Yes, yes.

00:06:43.917 --> 00:06:45.510
- And instead you just come

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:46.590
straight from the source, I guess.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.110
- Yes, and then I'm able to use

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.720
the extra time that I now
have during the interviews

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.783
to kind of drill in deeper to like,

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.860
the psychology behind the
feelings that they were having

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.290
and what things affected
them from those moments

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.550
and how they affect them now.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.380
And I guess it just allows me

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.390
to get a little bit deeper
with the conversations.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:09.633
- So the interviews are
like 20 to 40 minutes,

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.960
how much source footage do you have?

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.730
How much of it gets
cut out, do you reckon?

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:17.430
- Yeah, so it depends the person.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.640
Markiplier we shot like
two hours and 40 minutes

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.510
and we cut it down to a half
hour, somehow, miraculously.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.197
After it came out, he was like,

00:07:26.197 --> 00:07:28.422
"Holy shit, you did it, you bastard!"

00:07:28.422 --> 00:07:29.730
(all laughing)

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.580
But we apparently were able to get

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.830
all the key components,

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.980
the conversations that were really meaty,

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:40.080
have 'em in there while
cutting out all the fluff.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.143
And it's a lot of, there's a big process.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.097
We have five editors now.

00:07:47.097 --> 00:07:48.660
It goes from person to person,

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.090
they all have a very specific,

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.820
they're like a specific
cog in the machine.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.700
And it's a lot of just grinding it down.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:57.660
It goes to the first person,

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:00.270
they get the full 2 1/2 hours of footage.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:01.770
They cut it down to 50 and then it's like,

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.210
that person takes it and cuts it down,

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:05.460
and each person's really
good at refining the element.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.106
- We just have a one-man machine.

00:08:07.106 --> 00:08:08.430
(Garnt and Anthony laughing)

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.230
He works like 10 people's work.

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:11.970
He's just like, "I'll do it to myself."

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:13.830
- Yeah, damn, props to you!

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:15.720
- I do have a solution to that problem.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.150
If you're coming in with
like, 2 1/2 hours of footage

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.790
and having a hard time cutting it down,

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:23.730
have you ever thought about
this thing called a podcast?

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.456
- You mean just releasing it all unedited?

00:08:25.456 --> 00:08:26.528
(Garnt laughing)

00:08:26.528 --> 00:08:28.428
- Have you ever thought about no cuts?

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.590
- I have thought about
that, and people have asked,

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.090
they're like, "Come on,
just release the full thing,

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.370
I know you got it there somewhere!"

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.220
But I feel like I'm too
much of a stickler for like,

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:39.750
I want it to really be polished.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:41.967
I want everything that I come
out with to feel polished.

00:08:41.967 --> 00:08:43.320
And I know there's an environment

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.770
where people want longer
conversations that are unedited,

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.290
like this one obviously
has a huge audience.

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:50.760
Even on YouTube where I feel like people

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.820
are not generally looking
for very long uncut things,

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.963
you guys found that audience,
it clearly does exist.

00:08:56.963 --> 00:08:58.410
But I really want something

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.350
that feels a little bit more bite-size.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.840
- But you could take those parts

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:05.400
that you cut out for example,

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.630
turn it into a compilation,

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:09.270
release it on like, Patreon
for a behind the scenes,

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.230
have you though about doing that?

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:11.063
- For sure, for sure.

00:09:11.063 --> 00:09:13.560
We definitely have a bunch
of archived conversations

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:17.907
that we are noting, this
didn't really make the cut,

00:09:17.907 --> 00:09:19.823
but it is still a strong conversation.

00:09:19.823 --> 00:09:22.200
- I mean, with the amount of
questions I guess you're asking

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:23.250
and the amount of people you're asking,

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.500
I'm sure there's moments
