00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:46.320
if I'm going down in a direction

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:47.460
that you're not comfortable with.

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:49.110
But now that I do the pre-calls, again,

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:51.390
that removes most of that.

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:53.550
Sometimes I'll drill down
a little bit further,

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:55.650
but for the most part I
see if they're comfortable

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:57.660
and I try to word the questions in a way

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:00.690
where they can tell me
as much as they want

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.400
or go in as much as they want.

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:03.233
Sometimes they'll tell me something,

00:19:03.233 --> 00:19:04.680
and if I want to get in
deeper, I'll be like,

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:07.230
oh, and I'll kind of rephrase
what they just said to me.

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:11.460
Which sounds really stupid
if we just leave it in,

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:13.500
it's me saying the exact
same thing that they said.

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:14.790
But for some reason hearing it back

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:16.650
I feel like they know that I understood,

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:17.970
and then it gives them the opportunity

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:22.110
to refine or dig in deeper if they want.

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:24.510
So usually it's like I kind of
throw out little invitations

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:26.576
to go deeper without
feeling like I'm prying in.

00:19:26.576 --> 00:19:27.720
- Oh, okay.
- Right, right.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:30.990
So it's like, their choice
if they would like to go down

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:32.739
if they want to.
- Yes, yes, exactly.

00:19:32.739 --> 00:19:34.200
- Right, that's pretty smart to do.

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:36.275
- It sounded like you're
describing a different experience.

00:19:36.275 --> 00:19:38.858
(all laughing)

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:43.397
- I didn't mean for it
to come out like that!

00:19:43.397 --> 00:19:44.250
- You literally just took what he said

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:45.240
and explained it sexually,

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.969
like, so it's their choice to go down.

00:19:47.969 --> 00:19:50.662
- So you're telling me it's their choice?

00:19:50.662 --> 00:19:52.931
(all laughing)

00:19:52.931 --> 00:19:55.640
- They don't have to.

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:57.540
- So like, obviously,

00:19:57.540 --> 00:20:00.237
it's a massive interview
series on YouTube now,

00:20:00.237 --> 00:20:03.060
but you came off of this
off the back of Smosh.

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:05.730
What made you want to
start doing this series?

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:07.860
Did you want to explore a bunch of ideas

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:09.870
and then this was the
one that kind of stuck,

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:13.200
or was it always this
that you wanted to do?

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:18.200
- Well, I mean, Smosh was
definitely a comedic, silly thing,

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:19.920
so I never thought that I was gonna get

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:22.560
into any deeper conversations,

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:24.063
like connecting with people in the way

00:20:24.063 --> 00:20:26.580
that the series is now.

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:30.870
And the whole series kind
of started off as a joke.

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.480
Of course, the very
first video when I left,

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:34.800
I was like, okay.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:37.680
Part of the joke was that I'm
an independent YouTuber now,

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:39.750
I have no idea what's, I mean-

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:41.730
- I'm a strong, independent YouTuber.

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:43.920
- I was feeling myself.

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:46.440
And it was actually very true.

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:49.980
I had not uploaded my own video in years

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:51.990
because the company had taken it over.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:53.100
So part of the joke was like,

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:56.640
hey, what does a
independent YouTuber do now?

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:58.140
And I was asking for advice

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:01.800
and I interviewed a bunch
of popular YouTubers,

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:04.830
and there was something about that dynamic

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:06.390
of having a conversation

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:07.740
where I didn't know where it was going,

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:09.960
and the funniest moments were
the ones that were improvised

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:12.630
rather than 100% scripted.

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:13.890
And I really enjoyed that.

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:17.730
And then later on it
was like, let's revisit,

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:19.950
and I was struggling for a
long time, just floundering,

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:21.210
like, what do I do with this channel?

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:23.400
And every single week it was like,

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:24.270
I don't know what to do.

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:25.230
Okay, I figured it out.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:26.670
Okay, I shot it, okay, I hate it.

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:28.110
So now I'm gonna figure
something else out.

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:28.943
Okay, I find a shot,

00:21:28.943 --> 00:21:30.360
now I'm releasing this thing
that I don't really like,

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:31.800
but I ran outta time.

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:33.630
And eventually I was like,

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:35.880
what were the things
that I liked doing most

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:37.113
on the channel so far?

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:40.140
I'll dial in deeper with those ideas,

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:42.393
then I started expanding on it from there.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:44.790
- Man, Japan needs to start listening

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:45.870
to how you do your interviews,

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:48.120
'cause you would hate how
interviews are done in Japan.

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:49.950
- I don't think I've ever
heard a Japanese interview.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:50.783
Tell me about it.

00:21:50.783 --> 00:21:51.685
- Oh God!

00:21:51.685 --> 00:21:53.700
- Do you guys get interviewed on Japanese?

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.040
- No, so when did you do
interviews with anyone,

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.457
you have to give them the
exact script of the interview.

00:21:58.457 --> 00:22:00.630
- Oh!
- And there's no room for-

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:03.180
- Yeah, and they will
sometimes have scripted answers

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:05.400
about what they will say to you back.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:08.040
So everything is pre-planned.

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:11.700
You are not allowed to off script at all,

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:13.800
and it's just the most rigid interview

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:15.660
you could ever imagine.

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:18.270
- Is it like that in different
places of Asia as well?

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:22.450
Because I've also interviewed
someone from South Korea

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:25.563
and it was a similar thing where,

00:22:26.697 --> 00:22:28.487
well, they were speaking English to me

00:22:28.487 --> 00:22:30.570
and it was obviously not
their first language,

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:31.980
so it was like, of
course they want to know

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:33.660
what they're saying.
- That makes sense, yeah.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:36.390
- They wanted the questions
beforehand and I figured,

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:37.740
yeah, they wanna know
what I'm saying to them

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:40.290
just in case a word slips
by that they don't know.

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:42.450
But I didn't realize what they were doing

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:45.900
was scripting their
responses to such a degree

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:48.510
that afterwards they sent me their script.

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:50.613
- Geez!
- Oh wow!

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:53.580
- I thought it was a little
bit weird in the moment

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:54.990
whenever I'd ask a question,

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:58.469
they would repeat the answer
that they had already said.

00:22:58.469 --> 00:22:59.400
And I was like, "Hmm, okay,

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:01.230
well they were a lot more
loose in the pre-interview

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:03.531
just talking to me
about all these things,"

00:23:03.531 --> 00:23:04.364
and then here I was like,

00:23:04.364 --> 00:23:06.360
"Maybe they're just nervous or something.

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:08.100
Okay, I get it, people get nervous."

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:10.260
But then they sent me the
script and it was verbatim,

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:11.670
every single word that was said.

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:12.503
- Wow.

00:23:12.503 --> 00:23:13.590
- So I think they had a teleprompter.

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:14.885
- Yeah, I think like,

00:23:14.885 --> 00:23:16.380
'cause I have done quite
a bit of interviews

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:17.940
with industry people in Japan

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:18.997
and like a lot of them are like,

00:23:18.997 --> 00:23:21.780
"Okay, send me a list
of questions beforehand,

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:23.460
we will approve all the questions,

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:25.560
we will write up a very basic thing,"

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:26.820
and I'm like, "Okay, that's fine."

00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:28.447
But I've done enough
now where I'm just like,

00:23:28.447 --> 00:23:29.940
"Okay, we'll follow your script,

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:31.320
but I'm gonna add extra questions."

00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:32.407
- [Anthony] Yeah, yeah.

00:23:32.407 --> 00:23:34.170
- "And you won't be prepared for them,

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:35.700
and so I can really catch you out

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:36.870
on what you actually feel."

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:37.980
And for the most part,

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:39.900
they're okay with it
when it actually happens.

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:41.947
But I think if you tell them beforehand,

00:23:41.947 --> 00:23:44.400
"Hey, by the way, I'm gonna
throw in some extra questions,"

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:45.840
they'll just freak out.

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:47.040
'Cause they'll be like,
"We're not prepared for that."

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:48.000
- Their teams will say like-

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:49.560
- Yeah, their teams will be
like, "No, you can't do that."

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:51.690
But when I do it on the
day of the recording

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:54.030
and I get a good answer and a
good conversation flow going,

00:23:54.030 --> 00:23:56.160
then they're like, "Oh, maybe it's okay."

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:57.199
- [Anthony] Yeah, yeah.

00:23:57.199 --> 00:24:00.725
- Maybe it actually makes
for an interesting interview?

00:24:00.725 --> 00:24:01.740
- Maybe they're not out to fuck me over.

00:24:01.740 --> 00:24:02.910
- Yeah, exactly!

00:24:02.910 --> 00:24:05.190
Maybe they actually are just
genuinely interested in me

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:06.450
and want a good interview.

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:09.180
- I wonder how many people
have to be screwed over

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:11.071
in order for that to become the standard.

00:24:11.071 --> 00:24:12.150
- Yeah, it's like, how many bad interviews

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:13.840
must you have done to
need to get to that point.

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:15.360
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:18.510
- But it does take a while
to kind of build up that-

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:19.710
- Rapport.
- That rapport

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:24.710
and that level of respect or
trust with people, you know?

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:26.940
And I've seen that too.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:29.250
I have people that are
a lot more comfortable

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:30.510
with doing my interviews now

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:31.830
than I think that they
probably would've been

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:33.873
a couple years ago before I had really,

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:36.570
I don't know if I wanna
call it proven myself,

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:39.180
but like I guess proven
that I'm not going to

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:43.230
purposely and maliciously
try to defame someone

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:44.760
and ruin their career.

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:46.360
- You never really showcase even

00:24:47.335 --> 00:24:48.900
what most people would
consider strange things.

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:49.920
You never really show it as like,

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:51.240
whoa, look at this weird thing.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:52.980
You're just like, these are real people,

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:55.470
let's just see why real
people do what they do.

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:56.400
- If it is a strange thing,

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:58.710
I like to talk with them about
how it's a strange thing.

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:00.323
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:25:00.323 --> 00:25:04.230
- And the thing is, most
people are absolutely willing

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:06.930
to talk about how something
that they do is strange.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:10.620
It's not like they are
so in a different world

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:12.270
that they think everyone should do this,

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:13.920
and if they think it's
weird, then fuck you,

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:15.612
you know what I mean?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:25:15.612 --> 00:25:17.250
- But you've had people like that though,

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:18.083
right, in the past?

00:25:18.083 --> 00:25:19.230
- In which way?

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:21.990
- In the sense of them
trying to prove a point

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:23.940
and being like, "No, you're weird

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:25.530
for thinking that this
is weird," kind of thing.

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:27.600
- A little bit, but it still
seems a little playful.

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:29.629
They're like, "Normies don't get this."

00:25:29.629 --> 00:25:30.930
(all laughing)

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:33.570
So they still I think recognize that,

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:36.270
but definitely people are
a little bit defensive,

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:40.170
but I think it's because
they've been shown enough times

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:44.100
that they almost, they feel
like they have to be defensive,

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:45.600
'cause that means that
someone doesn't respect them

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:46.560
and I think it's just a lot of

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:48.843
like vying for respect of people.

00:25:50.138 --> 00:25:54.690
- Do you ever find people
that, it's a different field

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:56.130
where you're just talking
to someone casually,

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:58.200
but when you're in an
interview setting with cameras,

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:00.690
you really have to make
them feel comfortable,

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:03.000
like being themselves and talking.

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:04.920
Did you find that a challenge
with your interviews at all,

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:06.960
just getting people to
be comfortable on camera

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.100
as quick as possible?

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:10.380
- A little, yeah, and you
bring up a good point.

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:12.147
I do obviously have a limited time

00:26:12.147 --> 00:26:14.220
and I can't just spend the first

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:15.660
hour of the interview warming up.

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:17.160
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:21.960
- I go out of my way to
really connect with people.

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:23.130
We have the pre-interview call,

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:24.360
so there's a little bit of that dynamic

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:25.740
where we're already laughing together

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:27.060
and we already are on the same page,

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:28.140
so there's that when they show up,

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:30.390
but of course, they're
a little bit more tense.

00:26:30.390 --> 00:26:33.720
But I'll meet the guests on the street,

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:35.970
I'll walk them in, I'll
bring them out to the back,

00:26:35.970 --> 00:26:38.710
we do our COVID tests and we hang out

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:41.700
on this park bench that
I have on my balcony

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:45.450
and we just chit-chat
and I get to know them

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:47.703
and I tell them a lot about myself.

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:53.970
I think that there's a method
to kind of warming people up

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:56.430
so that they can see
like that you're genuine

00:26:56.430 --> 00:26:57.510
and that you you're not secretly

00:26:57.510 --> 00:26:58.830
gonna be trying to get a one up on them.

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:00.140
- We should have done that.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:00.973
(all laughing)

00:27:00.973 --> 00:27:02.613
- We should've taken him out back,

00:27:05.083 --> 00:27:06.786
let him know what's happening.
- So here's what we're about.

00:27:06.786 --> 00:27:07.860
- Damn, Anthony just
turned up and we were like,

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:08.693
all right, camera's on!

00:27:08.693 --> 00:27:10.920
- Let's go, come on, chop chop!

00:27:12.540 --> 00:27:13.620
- Now, we used to do something similar.

00:27:13.620 --> 00:27:14.910
We called it like, I dunno.

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:16.950
I dunno if it was a strat,
but the Denny's strat.

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:18.960
So there was a Denny's
outside of our office

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:22.280
and we often invite
them to lunch beforehand

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:24.450
to get to know them, have
a casual conversation.

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:27.030
But one issue we always
found ourselves having

00:27:27.030 --> 00:27:29.020
was we'd get into a conversation

00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:29.853
and it would just be like,

00:27:29.853 --> 00:27:31.859
shit, this is good material, this is good!

00:27:31.859 --> 00:27:32.692
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:27:32.692 --> 00:27:33.525
- I mean, it literally happened

00:27:33.525 --> 00:27:34.710
the five minutes since
after Anthony sat down

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:37.770
and we're like, shit, let's
just not ask any questions

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:40.650
'cause this is good material
that we can go through now.

00:27:40.650 --> 00:27:43.170
- Yeah, and that's part
of the challenge also.

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:45.960
I mean, the closer you get to someone,

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:47.040
the more you start connecting with them,

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:48.690
the more you start talking about things

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.180
that are then difficult to bring back up

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:53.217
and have it feel like the
first time you talked about it.

00:27:53.217 --> 00:27:56.760
But that's what helps
having my questions already.

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:58.350
Because we actually go away

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:00.150
from those conversations
for a little while,

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:03.390
and then if we have a good conversation,

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:04.410
I'll write it down in my notes.

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:05.310
I'll be like, this is good,

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:06.810
we need to bring this back up again.

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:08.100
- Right.
- That's a good idea.

00:28:08.100 --> 00:28:09.493
- Smart, we should take notes.

00:28:09.493 --> 00:28:10.546
(all laughing)

00:28:10.546 --> 00:28:11.530
- We should definitely take notes, yeah!

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:12.363
- This isn't a podcast,

00:28:12.363 --> 00:28:14.313
we're literally just
asking for advice now.

00:28:15.313 --> 00:28:17.239
- It's like, how do you do it?

00:28:17.239 --> 00:28:18.853
- I could have just sent you a message.

00:28:18.853 --> 00:28:20.370
(all laughing)

00:28:20.370 --> 00:28:21.970
- It's like, where were
the questions, bro?

00:28:21.970 --> 00:28:23.970
- Yeah, no, this is great.

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:24.803
- So we're talking about before,

00:28:24.803 --> 00:28:27.030
like how many people you have
to like run this show now.

00:28:27.030 --> 00:28:29.460
So how many people are
there behind the scenes

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:30.990
and how many did you start off with?

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:32.180
- Yes.

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:35.310
So I started off with
just two camera operators

00:28:35.310 --> 00:28:36.870
and it was just myself
trying to figure out

00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:38.940
what the fuck I was doing.
- Right.

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:41.670
- And then I slowly but surely

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:44.130
had new people come on to help me

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:48.300
and now I have 10 employees on the show.

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:49.133
- Wow.
- Jesus.

00:28:49.133 --> 00:28:50.250
- Very nice.

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:52.140
- You know, it doesn't sound
like that big of a number

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:55.382
until you think about how much
everyone needs to be paid.

00:28:55.382 --> 00:28:57.267
(all laughing)

00:28:57.267 --> 00:28:58.472
- [Connor] That's a lot.

00:28:58.472 --> 00:29:00.115
- Yeah, that's a lot, that's a lot.

00:29:00.115 --> 00:29:03.003
- And there's a lot of moving parts.

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:09.720
I started, I took two
years of community college

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:13.503
and I started to take business
administration classes.

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:17.262
Smosh started happening 2005

00:29:17.262 --> 00:29:18.750
and I was like, ah, fuck college.

00:29:18.750 --> 00:29:23.070
But I realized I've actually
always been interested

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:26.880
in kind of managing a team and a business,

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:30.150
and I think it's a lot of fun, teaching.

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:31.710
And especially once you could teach,

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:34.260
like I have a head of
post, the main editor,

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:36.120
and I get to teach him all the things

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:37.560
and we get on the same page.

00:29:37.560 --> 00:29:39.633
And then there's four people that work,

00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:42.960
I guess below him is
how people would say it,

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:44.427
but that work with him.

00:29:44.427 --> 00:29:46.230
And so I get to communicate
with one person,

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:47.460
he communicates with four.

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:50.400
So I just really enjoy the dynamic

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:52.260
of a whole team working together

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:54.844
and having it feel like
a well-oiled machine.

00:29:54.844 --> 00:29:56.373
- 'Cause I was gonna ask,

00:29:57.270 --> 00:29:59.310
how many people did you have at most

00:29:59.310 --> 00:30:01.110
when you were at Smosh working?

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:01.943
- [Anthony] Oh my God.

00:30:01.943 --> 00:30:03.570
- How many employees did you have then?

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:04.710
- It's kind of hard to say

00:30:04.710 --> 00:30:08.280
because Defy owned Smosh

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:13.280
and they I think had 30
people working with Smosh

00:30:14.106 --> 00:30:18.360
and all the side channels and
the live events and the live,

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:20.490
yeah, there's so many elements there

00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:23.163
that I guess 30 was the magic number.

00:30:24.088 --> 00:30:25.590
I don't even think I met

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:27.070
some of the people that
were working there.

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:28.059
(Connor, Joey, and Garnt laughing)

00:30:28.059 --> 00:30:29.370
- It's like, they're there somewhere.

00:30:29.370 --> 00:30:31.440
- It was a weird team situation

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.960
where we all never really
got to know each other fully.

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:35.550
- Obviously, we're YouTubers,

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:37.350
you see that lovely 1 out of 10 system,

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:38.430
which is the most
depressing system on earth

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:39.966
and it's very Black Mirror-esque.

00:30:39.966 --> 00:30:42.357
- Or it's the best system in
the world when it's 1 outta 10.

00:30:42.357 --> 00:30:43.727
- Our viewers have heard
us complain about it

00:30:43.727 --> 00:30:45.030
like a million times,

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:47.640
but I imagine when you have 10 employees,

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:49.707
that suddenly if you're
getting a 10 out of 10

00:30:49.707 --> 00:30:52.025
and then maybe you get a 9 out of 10,

00:30:52.025 --> 00:30:52.950
it starts to get a lot scarier, right?

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:54.933
- Yeah, there's a lot more weight.

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:57.874
- I mean, how do you deal with that,

00:30:57.874 --> 00:30:58.857
did you deal with it like
as a team or you like,

00:30:58.857 --> 00:31:01.676
"Guys should leave me alone,
I'm gonna cry a moment"?

00:31:01.676 --> 00:31:03.930
(Joey, Anthony, and Garnt laughing)

00:31:03.930 --> 00:31:06.420
- There's still a part of
me that is affected by it,

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:08.493
I can't say that there's not.

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:12.120
And it's not even like
an outward thing of like,

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:14.250
ah shit, dude, what am I gonna do?

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:16.380
But even if I just carry on with my day,

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:18.720
there is like a part of me where my energy

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:20.250
feels a little bit like something

00:31:20.250 --> 00:31:22.290
was pulled out from me a little bit.

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:23.640
Even if I'm not thinking about it,

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:25.635
it must be some suppression.

00:31:25.635 --> 00:31:27.597
And then when there is like a 1 out of 10,

00:31:27.597 --> 00:31:30.483
I'm a little, oh, my
posture's a little better.

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:35.220
But I've been really
working on reminding myself

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:40.110
that a bad video now was
actually a good video for me,

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:43.290
like in terms of performance.

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:46.050
When I first started
what I would now consider

00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:47.640
probably a lower performing video

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:50.010
was a good performing video,
and we still have sponsors.

00:31:50.010 --> 00:31:55.010
And I try to look at
what my actual progress

00:31:55.110 --> 00:31:58.410
has been for the show,
it's not just views.

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:01.680
It's so easy to think views
are a measure of progress

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:03.270
because it's the biggest number.

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:04.170
When you go on YouTube,

00:32:04.170 --> 00:32:06.210
it's attached to every single thumbnail.

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:07.740
In fact, many people decide

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:09.240
if they're gonna click on something

00:32:09.240 --> 00:32:10.973
based on how high that number is.

00:32:10.973 --> 00:32:15.270
You see one video and it has 10,000 views,

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:16.920
someone is much less likely then

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:19.350
to click that same video
with 10 million views

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:21.420
because there's that curiosity aspect of,

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:24.033
wait, so many people are watching this,

00:32:24.033 --> 00:32:27.000
this has gotta be something
extraordinary, right?

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:29.310
- If there is a YouTube
video under 30 seconds

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:30.604
that has over a million views,

00:32:30.604 --> 00:32:31.437
you know it's a banger!

00:32:31.437 --> 00:32:32.709
- You know you're gonna click it!

00:32:32.709 --> 00:32:34.662
- You notice that, it's
always a fucking banger!

00:32:34.662 --> 00:32:36.914
- And that shit's gonna be
delivered to you so quickly,

00:32:36.914 --> 00:32:39.405
30 seconds, that's it?

00:32:39.405 --> 00:32:40.440
30 seconds to have a banger.

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:43.140
- Instant serotonin.
- I'm fine with that.

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:45.840
But, you know, that is not my only measure

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:48.300
of where I'm going with this.

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:49.713
Part of it is also like,

00:32:51.810 --> 00:32:55.320
I want to grow respect
and have people trust

00:32:55.320 --> 00:32:57.570
that I will treat them
with respect on the show.

00:32:57.570 --> 00:33:01.860
So I'm having people, huge name people

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:03.967
have started to reach
out to me and say like,

00:33:03.967 --> 00:33:05.730
"Ooh, I'd love to be
on the show sometime,"

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:07.740
and that's something
that I never had before.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:09.990
So regardless of where my numbers go,

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:13.080
I have people that I really
respect reaching out to me

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:14.490
and wanting to talk with me

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:16.200
and have that experience
of being on the show,

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:18.297
and that is something that is so cool

00:33:18.297 --> 00:33:21.270
and I could clearly see kind
of that's my metric now.

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:22.642
- That's impact that you're
having in the greater community.

00:33:22.642 --> 00:33:23.475
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:33:23.475 --> 00:33:25.770
- I mean, so the whole YouTube system

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:27.120
and giving a shit about analytics,

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:28.657
was that something that I suppose

00:33:28.657 --> 00:33:30.180
that Smosh under Defy Media

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:32.310
that you never concerned yourself with?

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:34.260
- I didn't really, because
we were just on a salary.

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:35.093
- Right, right, right.

00:33:35.093 --> 00:33:37.393
- So I was like, "These
numbers don't affect me."

00:33:37.393 --> 00:33:38.226
- Yeah, and Defy came in there like,

00:33:38.226 --> 00:33:40.260
"Anthony, man, it's not doing too hot."

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:41.297
- I'm like, "Oh my God, it looks like

00:33:41.297 --> 00:33:43.167
I have two more years, so."

00:33:44.700 --> 00:33:47.160
- So it must have been
you were under Smosh,

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:49.920
then under Defy Media and
then now you're separate,

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:51.000
so there's probably that big gap

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:53.100
where you didn't really take notice

00:33:53.100 --> 00:33:54.320
of what was really
happening on YouTube much.

00:33:54.320 --> 00:33:56.490
- Yeah, yeah.
- Wow, that's interesting.

00:33:56.490 --> 00:33:58.264
- So now I had to start
learning all these things.

00:33:58.264 --> 00:33:59.507
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:33:59.507 --> 00:34:01.650
- When did you kind of tune out of

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.570
giving a fuck about what
was happening on YouTube

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:06.630
or kind of not paying as much
attention and just doing-

00:34:06.630 --> 00:34:07.600
- You mean like when I was Defy?

00:34:07.600 --> 00:34:09.030
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:34:09.030 --> 00:34:14.030
- I'd say it was probably
around 2014 or 2015 probably

00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:16.860
to 2017 when I eventually left

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:18.513
and decided to do my own thing.

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:23.340
I think it was the two years
that I was unhappy with

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:25.020
where things were going

00:34:25.020 --> 00:34:27.150
and the way that the company was being ran

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:28.530
and the decisions that were being made

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:29.880
and how I was being left in the dark

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:32.730
about a lot of decisions
that were really important

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:33.960
that were being made.

00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:36.120
And it was around then that I started

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:39.090
to just kind of tune out
from YouTube in general.

00:34:39.090 --> 00:34:41.790
I would still put a lot of my heart

00:34:41.790 --> 00:34:43.860
into making the things that
I was making at that time,

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:45.480
but there was still this part of me

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:47.070
that was just getting detached from it,

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:48.360
and that's when I kind of stopped caring

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:49.950
about browsing YouTube

00:34:49.950 --> 00:34:52.590
and being part of the community as much.

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:53.460
- Just when you thought you were out,

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:54.293
you got dragged right back in.

00:34:54.293 --> 00:34:56.640
- Yeah, yeah, I almost
made it outta the rat race!

00:34:56.640 --> 00:34:58.920
- So do you think not caring about it

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:01.830
gave you more or less
freedom than you do now?

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.200
Because now you do have to
care about the algorithm,

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:05.250
every YouTuber has to care

00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:06.780
about the algorithm unfortunately.

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:10.290
- I think it's kind of about
caring about the right things,

00:35:10.290 --> 00:35:11.820
and by the right things,

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:13.500
it's not to say there's a
wrong thing to care about,

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:17.040
but the figuring out what
is meaningful for you

00:35:17.040 --> 00:35:18.989
and then caring about those things.

00:35:18.989 --> 00:35:22.020
It's so easy to start
caring about the things

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:23.460
that seem like they matter most,

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:25.882
like just straight up view count,

00:35:25.882 --> 00:35:28.620
but then you could drill in,
you could read the comments

00:35:28.620 --> 00:35:31.740
and see how much of an
impact a certain thing made

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:33.900
and you could realize that
that is more important.

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:37.110
So you have to define the
things that matter most to you

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:38.940
before you just start
caring about everything.

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:40.770
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Damn.

00:35:40.770 --> 00:35:43.260
- So you mentioned something
earlier just off the cuff,

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:45.473
but you were just like, "Yeah,
so I quit college in 2005

00:35:45.473 --> 00:35:47.400
because Smosh was a thing."

00:35:47.400 --> 00:35:49.130
- Yeah.
- What!

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:51.930
- So you quit in college
in 2005 to do YouTube?

00:35:51.930 --> 00:35:52.980
- Yeah.

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:55.562
- Wow, that's super,
super ahead of the curve.

00:35:55.562 --> 00:35:58.288
- Yeah, that was really
ahead of the curve!

00:35:58.288 --> 00:35:59.457
You like, foresaw that shit.

00:35:59.457 --> 00:36:02.329
- "Mom, I made one Pokemon Dollar,

00:36:02.329 --> 00:36:04.320
I'm going, I'm quitting!"

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:05.493
- Basically.

00:36:05.493 --> 00:36:06.930
(all laughing)

00:36:06.930 --> 00:36:08.040
- I said it a little wrong.

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:09.780
So I went to two full semesters,

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:11.550
so I think it was technically 2006.

00:36:11.550 --> 00:36:14.190
And I was signing up for classes

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:17.190
and I was thinking about
the amount of time,

00:36:17.190 --> 00:36:18.780
'cause the classes obviously get harder

00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:23.370
the further along you
get with your degree,

00:36:23.370 --> 00:36:26.100
'cause they start dialing into
the actual difficult things.

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:27.870
And I was like, okay, I could be doing

00:36:27.870 --> 00:36:30.693
all these extremely
difficult things for this,

00:36:32.700 --> 00:36:35.070
which was now taking my time
away from this other thing

00:36:35.070 --> 00:36:36.510
that is now starting to build,

00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:38.583
this is a once in a lifetime opportunity.

00:36:41.100 --> 00:36:42.060
I felt like I was almost like

00:36:42.060 --> 00:36:44.130
the master of my own fate with this world

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:46.590
while I felt like
getting a college degree,

00:36:46.590 --> 00:36:50.310
while obviously super
helpful for most people,

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:53.790
I felt like this is something
I can always come back to.

00:36:53.790 --> 00:36:56.280
And also this kind of
puts me in a position

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:59.460
where I'm kind of following a system

00:36:59.460 --> 00:37:00.810
that other people have
put in place for me.

00:37:00.810 --> 00:37:04.603
I felt like I was making
up my own rules on YouTube.

00:37:04.603 --> 00:37:08.160
- So did you foresee the fucking ecosystem

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:09.359
YouTube would become, or you're just like-

00:37:09.359 --> 00:37:12.020
- Fuck, no, no!

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:14.603
Yeah, I'm like, "Ah, I see it all!"

00:37:14.603 --> 00:37:15.862
- Well you were confident
enough to be like,

00:37:15.862 --> 00:37:18.413
bye college, you know?

00:37:18.413 --> 00:37:20.550
- Did you just have this
like, 'cause obviously,

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:23.343
how many videos or where
were you at at that point

00:37:23.343 --> 00:37:25.350
when you decided to quit college?

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:26.280
What kinda stage was it at?

00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:30.300
- I think we had. (laughs)

00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:31.875
- How many?

00:37:31.875 --> 00:37:33.182
- I think we had six videos out.

00:37:33.182 --> 00:37:34.015
(all laughing)

00:37:34.015 --> 00:37:36.348
- What the fuck!
- Oh my God!

00:37:38.299 --> 00:37:39.930
- Would you ever recommend to
a YouTuber with six videos-

00:37:39.930 --> 00:37:42.737
- No, never do what I did.

00:37:42.737 --> 00:37:44.220
Never do what I do,

00:37:44.220 --> 00:37:46.410
unless your parents are very well off

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:48.027
and they're totally fine
with you not doing college

00:37:48.027 --> 00:37:49.320
and they wanna help you.

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:50.970
- How many subs did
you have when you quit?

00:37:50.970 --> 00:37:52.776
- The subs were not a thing.

00:37:52.776 --> 00:37:53.623
- Oh, they weren't a thing?

00:37:53.623 --> 00:37:56.014
- They were called followers.
- Wow!

00:37:56.014 --> 00:37:57.900
(all laughing)

00:37:57.900 --> 00:37:58.733
They were not a thing.

00:37:58.733 --> 00:38:00.810
But the reason that I felt confident

00:38:00.810 --> 00:38:02.220
is because this outside company

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:05.280
that was competing with
YouTube called (beep),

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:07.230
does not exist anymore,

00:38:07.230 --> 00:38:09.690
they reached out to us and they were like,

00:38:09.690 --> 00:38:10.980
they gave us this contract

00:38:10.980 --> 00:38:13.200
that was in hindsight very shitty,

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.450
but we were college kids

00:38:15.450 --> 00:38:17.280
who were still living
under our parents' roof

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:18.420
and we were like, you know what?

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:19.560
This makes sense.

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:22.710
We can fund these things
that we love doing.

00:38:22.710 --> 00:38:24.720
We just had the best time hanging out

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:28.003
and just laughing our
asses off, creating things.

00:38:28.003 --> 00:38:29.220
A college kid, that's
what the college kids

00:38:29.220 --> 00:38:31.950
really want to do most of
the time, not the homework!

00:38:31.950 --> 00:38:33.691
- Man, you get one
paycheck and you're like,

00:38:33.691 --> 00:38:36.036
"I am set for life!

00:38:36.036 --> 00:38:38.220
Mother, I have a career now!"

