had a little button that said,

00:48:23.460 --> 00:48:24.510
are you sure you want to subscribe?

00:48:24.510 --> 00:48:26.969
- Was that back when you
could actually talk to YouTube

00:48:26.969 --> 00:48:29.040
and get through to them?
- Yes, yes!

00:48:29.040 --> 00:48:31.680
Especially if you had a
big audience on YouTube,

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:33.660
it was like concierge service.
- Really?

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:35.250
You just kind of get shit done?

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:37.590
- Oh yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Wow.

00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:40.650
- We went and hung out in, I
don't know, I don't remember

00:48:40.650 --> 00:48:43.653
I don't know San Bruno,
San Jose, some San,

00:48:44.733 --> 00:48:46.470
near San Francisco,

00:48:46.470 --> 00:48:49.347
and hung out with people
there that work there.

00:48:49.347 --> 00:48:53.160
And then Chad Hurley, Steve
Chen, the creators of YouTube.

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:54.873
- [Joey] Damn, dude!

00:48:54.873 --> 00:48:57.000
- We just don't have enough subscribers.

00:48:57.000 --> 00:48:58.440
- Think of the good old days, right?

00:48:58.440 --> 00:49:01.290
- It was so rare though for
people to have an audience,

00:49:01.290 --> 00:49:04.196
because most people that
uploaded videos to YouTube-

00:49:04.196 --> 00:49:05.029
- One-off viral clips and stuff.

00:49:05.029 --> 00:49:06.390
- Were one-off viral clips,

00:49:06.390 --> 00:49:08.820
so it was very rare for
people to have audiences

00:49:08.820 --> 00:49:12.750
and have recurring videos
of a specific channel.

00:49:12.750 --> 00:49:15.000
So I think that they really
treated everyone back then

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:17.350
as like their gems that
were making YouTube

00:49:19.204 --> 00:49:20.037
be something bigger.

00:49:20.037 --> 00:49:21.750
- 'Cause I remember back in the day

00:49:21.750 --> 00:49:23.640
it was just like you would
go on the most viewed,

00:49:23.640 --> 00:49:25.137
you go on like the most
viewed of all time,

00:49:25.137 --> 00:49:26.370
and obviously the Pokemon
lip sync was there,

00:49:26.370 --> 00:49:28.770
then you have like "Evolution of Dance."

00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:30.420
- Dude, ah, classics!

00:49:30.420 --> 00:49:31.768
- Fucking "Charlie Bit My Finger."

00:49:31.768 --> 00:49:33.111
- Classics!
- Classics.

00:49:33.111 --> 00:49:34.200
- The classics, you know, the classics.

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:36.780
- Because I always forget, at one point

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:38.920
you were the most subscribed
channel on the platform.

00:49:38.920 --> 00:49:40.650
- Yeah, yeah, two separate times.

00:49:40.650 --> 00:49:41.483
- Two separate times, yeah.

00:49:41.483 --> 00:49:42.870
- It was like a three-month period

00:49:42.870 --> 00:49:44.711
and a eight-month period
or something, I forget.

00:49:44.711 --> 00:49:45.544
- How was that?

00:49:45.544 --> 00:49:48.020
How was it at that at one
point you were at the top?

00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:49.683
- It was really cool.

00:49:49.683 --> 00:49:50.797
- Was there pressure though, I bet?

00:49:50.797 --> 00:49:52.470
- There was definitely pressure.

00:49:52.470 --> 00:49:56.880
And it filtered the way that I looked at

00:49:56.880 --> 00:49:59.790
everything that I did for better or worse.

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:01.740
There was the good of like,

00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:03.510
ooh, everything that I make
has some kind of value,

00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:05.010
I should put a lot of my
time and energy in this

00:50:05.010 --> 00:50:06.197
'cause I know a lot of
people are gonna see it

00:50:06.197 --> 00:50:08.100
and it really pushed me to constantly

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:09.720
be pushing myself outside my boundary

00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:10.950
or out of my comfort zone

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:14.430
because I knew that it
would, not literally pay off,

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:15.300
but it would pay off in the sense

00:50:15.300 --> 00:50:16.830
that a lot of people would see it

00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:17.900
and potentially be able to enjoy it.

00:50:17.900 --> 00:50:20.280
But then on the opposite side of that,

00:50:20.280 --> 00:50:22.410
there was a lot of getting in my own head

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:26.310
about the pressure and how
I have to live up to this.

00:50:26.310 --> 00:50:28.950
And then you're also
always seeing comments

00:50:28.950 --> 00:50:31.200
of people saying like,
how is this, whatever,

00:50:31.200 --> 00:50:34.230
and it's a lot of filtering
it out to be like,

00:50:34.230 --> 00:50:35.910
it doesn't matter that some
people don't like this,

00:50:35.910 --> 00:50:37.110
it's not for them,

00:50:37.110 --> 00:50:39.210
but it would get in my head of like,

00:50:39.210 --> 00:50:40.500
oh, I need to be better

00:50:40.500 --> 00:50:42.991
and it was a lot of like
this, this pressure.

00:50:42.991 --> 00:50:44.250
- You were mentioning earlier

00:50:44.250 --> 00:50:45.510
about how obviously when you started,

00:50:45.510 --> 00:50:48.090
you got a deal from a company.
- Yeah.

00:50:48.090 --> 00:50:50.460
- I imagine when it
was starting to ramp up

00:50:50.460 --> 00:50:52.140
and get to number one most subscribed,

00:50:52.140 --> 00:50:53.310
you're probably getting offered

00:50:53.310 --> 00:50:57.420
all sorts of weird and predatory stuff

00:50:57.420 --> 00:50:59.370
and just probably just the most stressful

00:50:59.370 --> 00:51:01.140
company meetings ever.

00:51:01.140 --> 00:51:05.730
- Dude, we had someone
show up to my house,

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:07.440
I was living with my mom, obviously,

00:51:07.440 --> 00:51:09.480
like in just our small little, tiny house

00:51:09.480 --> 00:51:12.180
outside of Sacramento, California.

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:14.280
And there was this predatory,

00:51:14.280 --> 00:51:15.840
I don't wanna call him predatory,

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:17.820
but there's this guy came
up in a business suit

00:51:17.820 --> 00:51:20.190
and he's like, knocked on the door.

00:51:20.190 --> 00:51:22.576
My mom was like, "There's
someone outside to see you."

00:51:22.576 --> 00:51:23.640
- Oh no!

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:28.530
- So Ian and I walk out and
we're like, "What is up?"

00:51:28.530 --> 00:51:30.780
And he is like, "Oh, I got your address

00:51:30.780 --> 00:51:32.760
because I saw that you just
registered for a business

00:51:32.760 --> 00:51:33.900
and it was marked under this address,

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:36.450
so I figured I would just show
up and tell you a few things

00:51:36.450 --> 00:51:37.743
about what you could be doing,
- What the fuck!

00:51:37.743 --> 00:51:40.650
- how you could be monetizing this."

00:51:40.650 --> 00:51:41.583
And I was like,

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:44.203
I was 18, so I didn't know,

00:51:44.203 --> 00:51:48.060
and it was my first time
with any kind of fame

00:51:48.060 --> 00:51:49.380
or notoriety or anything.

00:51:49.380 --> 00:51:51.360
I didn't know that this was
a fucked up thing to do,

00:51:51.360 --> 00:51:52.623
so I entertained it-

00:51:54.180 --> 00:51:55.780
- Come on inside.
- Would you like some tea?

00:51:55.780 --> 00:51:57.450
- Okay, I drew the line there,

00:51:57.450 --> 00:52:01.143
I didn't invite him inside,
there was no invitations inside.

00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:04.440
But luckily he was a vampire

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:06.940
and he could not enter
without me inviting him in.

00:52:08.340 --> 00:52:11.190
No, so he was like, "Okay,

00:52:11.190 --> 00:52:14.337
so your "Pokemon Theme Song" music video,"

00:52:15.258 --> 00:52:17.850
(all laughing)

00:52:17.850 --> 00:52:21.990
like it's this epic
piece of cinematic work.

00:52:21.990 --> 00:52:25.260
And he was like, "It has 3 million views,

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:27.060
so think about it this way.

00:52:27.060 --> 00:52:30.570
I could get you sponsored by Coca-Cola."

00:52:30.570 --> 00:52:31.403
- Oh my God.

00:52:31.403 --> 00:52:35.550
- "And if you get $1,000 per million,

00:52:35.550 --> 00:52:38.337
that gets $3,000 in
your pocket like that."

00:52:38.337 --> 00:52:40.530
And I was like, "First of all,

00:52:40.530 --> 00:52:44.910
do you have any contact with Coca-Cola?"

00:52:44.910 --> 00:52:46.020
And he just started listing it all

00:52:46.020 --> 00:52:48.047
and I realized immediately
that he was full of shit,

00:52:48.047 --> 00:52:50.130
he was trying to make
some kind of a connection.

00:52:50.130 --> 00:52:52.020
And he was like, "But then McDonald's,"

00:52:52.020 --> 00:52:53.250
and I was like, "dude,"

00:52:53.250 --> 00:52:55.020
so he clearly didn't know
what he was talking about.

00:52:55.020 --> 00:52:58.363
Luckily he only showed up
one other time after that.

00:52:58.363 --> 00:52:59.940
- Only one other time!
(all laughing)

00:52:59.940 --> 00:53:01.297
- This one other time I was like,

00:53:01.297 --> 00:53:02.520
"Mom, when that guy shows up again,

00:53:02.520 --> 00:53:03.770
you just ignore that man.

00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:06.480
The bad man in the suit, leave him."

00:53:08.070 --> 00:53:11.910
But luckily we were, I mean,
we were not very smart,

00:53:11.910 --> 00:53:13.050
but we were smart enough to know

00:53:13.050 --> 00:53:14.500
that that did not feel right.

00:53:15.690 --> 00:53:16.840
- 'Cause we were talking to Sean,

00:53:16.840 --> 00:53:18.690
Jacksepticeye, a few weeks ago

00:53:18.690 --> 00:53:20.740
and how back in like the
early days of YouTube,

00:53:20.740 --> 00:53:24.570
there was just no boundaries
between fans and the creator,

00:53:24.570 --> 00:53:27.074
so fans would literally
show up at his house.

00:53:27.074 --> 00:53:27.907
- Oh yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:53:27.907 --> 00:53:28.740
- Did that happen to you a lot?

00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:29.573
- Oh, absolutely.

00:53:29.573 --> 00:53:32.040
- Yeah, yeah, so Ian and I ended up

00:53:32.040 --> 00:53:34.920
renting this place together he and I,

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:36.780
and we had another roommate with us too,

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:39.600
and we lived there for I think four years,

00:53:39.600 --> 00:53:40.470
and that's when our videos

00:53:40.470 --> 00:53:42.510
were really, really, really
starting to become popular,

00:53:42.510 --> 00:53:46.590
like 2009 to 2011-ish.

00:53:46.590 --> 00:53:50.070
And there's so many ridiculous stories

00:53:50.070 --> 00:53:52.500
of like, mobs of people,

00:53:52.500 --> 00:53:54.120
I'm trying to edit in my editing room

00:53:54.120 --> 00:53:56.670
and I just hear people
running around the house

00:53:56.670 --> 00:53:59.910
and laughing and looking
through our trash.

00:53:59.910 --> 00:54:01.247
- What the fuck!
- What!

00:54:01.247 --> 00:54:03.174
- People did not know boundaries.

00:54:03.174 --> 00:54:04.470
- How did they find out where you live?

00:54:04.470 --> 00:54:06.481
- Because we were really stupid.

00:54:06.481 --> 00:54:07.314
(Connor, Joey, and Garnt laughing)

00:54:07.314 --> 00:54:08.820
So all blame is on us,

00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:11.193
but we would just shoot
outside of our house,

00:54:12.141 --> 00:54:15.990
our address would be in
it, our cross streets,

00:54:15.990 --> 00:54:18.950
like they knew exactly how to find us.

00:54:18.950 --> 00:54:20.160
- Because this was also during the time

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:21.600
where you were doing the mail unboxings

00:54:21.600 --> 00:54:22.650
on the second channel, right?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:54:22.650 --> 00:54:26.606
- So I bet that also came
into it as well, right?

00:54:26.606 --> 00:54:30.060
- I remember like, back way
back when I used to watch

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:30.893
those mail unboxings.
- Oh yeah?

00:54:30.893 --> 00:54:32.279
- Those were my favorites,

00:54:32.279 --> 00:54:34.140
I love those videos.
- Dude, those were so wild!

00:54:34.140 --> 00:54:37.230
- I think at that time it
was something so unique

00:54:37.230 --> 00:54:40.620
because obviously you know
big, massive celebrities

00:54:40.620 --> 00:54:41.880
would be getting this kind of stuff,

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:44.970
but I think it was the first
time that a normal person

00:54:44.970 --> 00:54:48.390
could just watch somebody
receive all sorts of weird shit.

00:54:48.390 --> 00:54:49.968
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:54:49.968 --> 00:54:51.650
- And it was just like, this is so weird.

00:54:51.650 --> 00:54:55.284
- It really did show you
the scope of how strange

00:54:55.284 --> 00:54:57.000
the common person can be

00:54:57.000 --> 00:54:59.277
with the items that
they choose to send you

00:54:59.277 --> 00:55:01.950
and the drawings that they choose to draw.

00:55:01.950 --> 00:55:03.030
- That must have been weird.

00:55:03.030 --> 00:55:03.870
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:55:03.870 --> 00:55:05.713
- What was some of the weirdest
stuff you ever got sent?

00:55:05.713 --> 00:55:08.528
- I have some very good go-tos
for that, don't you worry.

00:55:08.528 --> 00:55:09.843
- Okay!
- We got it, yes!

00:55:09.843 --> 00:55:10.676
- Yes!

00:55:10.676 --> 00:55:14.026
- We we got Ziploc bag of dead bees.

00:55:14.026 --> 00:55:15.870
- (laughing) What!

00:55:15.870 --> 00:55:18.667
- I wanna know, how did
they collect those bees?

00:55:18.667 --> 00:55:19.500
- They might not have been bees.

00:55:19.500 --> 00:55:20.970
Maybe they look like bees,

00:55:20.970 --> 00:55:24.330
I did not examine enough to know for sure.

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:29.330
We have a slightly smaller,
thinner bag of nail clippings.

00:55:30.766 --> 00:55:32.760
- [Connor, Joey, And Garnt] Oh!

00:55:32.760 --> 00:55:35.430
- Didn't you receive shit
as well at one point?

00:55:35.430 --> 00:55:37.560
- We received stuff that smelled like shit

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:39.707
that we never ventured into.
(Connor laughing)

00:55:39.707 --> 00:55:41.106
- You didn't play with it?

00:55:41.106 --> 00:55:43.770
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, I felt the texture.

00:55:43.770 --> 00:55:45.789
It's like, "This is
definitely elephant shit."

00:55:45.789 --> 00:55:46.703
- Gave it a little smell?

00:55:48.173 --> 00:55:50.760
- It's definitely human shit.

00:55:50.760 --> 00:55:52.410
- We definitely had boxes,

00:55:52.410 --> 00:55:54.810
and we received so many
boxes that we would sometimes

00:55:54.810 --> 00:55:55.680
just throw it in our garage

00:55:55.680 --> 00:55:59.040
and they would sit in
the humidity and the heat

00:55:59.040 --> 00:56:01.950
and it would be months
before we'd get to it.

00:56:01.950 --> 00:56:03.780
And people thought it was so funny

00:56:03.780 --> 00:56:07.260
that they would send us sandwiches.

00:56:07.260 --> 00:56:10.713
And these sandwiches
were just rot and mold.

00:56:10.713 --> 00:56:13.080
- Oh, 'cause of like the
food fight stuff, I imagine.

00:56:13.080 --> 00:56:16.080
- I don't know of why,
but it became a thing.

00:56:16.080 --> 00:56:19.110
And the bags, the Ziploc
bags would inflate

00:56:19.110 --> 00:56:21.630
with the gases from these
things and they would pop,

00:56:21.630 --> 00:56:23.550
and then the scent would just permeate

00:56:23.550 --> 00:56:26.010
throughout our entire house.
- Oh God.

00:56:26.010 --> 00:56:27.450
- Yeah, you you'd throw away the boxes

00:56:27.450 --> 00:56:28.890
once they were kind of-

00:56:28.890 --> 00:56:30.060
- You could see like, patches?

00:56:30.060 --> 00:56:32.643
- Kinda sopping wet or oily or greasy.

00:56:33.480 --> 00:56:35.340
But that quickly was like,

00:56:35.340 --> 00:56:38.670
it's probably about time
to stop receiving the mail.

00:56:38.670 --> 00:56:40.700
Like, it was fun, it
was a great time period.

00:56:40.700 --> 00:56:42.240
- So how long did you guys do that for?

00:56:42.240 --> 00:56:43.770
- I think we did it for
like three or four years.

00:56:43.770 --> 00:56:44.940
- Oh my God!

00:56:44.940 --> 00:56:46.441
- Yeah, yeah.
- Was your garage just fucked?

00:56:46.441 --> 00:56:47.593
- And by the end it was
years of stuff just built up,

00:56:47.593 --> 00:56:50.340
towers and towers of mail.
- Oh my God.

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:51.450
- But like you said, every now and then

00:56:51.450 --> 00:56:52.770
you probably get something
really fucking cool

00:56:52.770 --> 00:56:54.708
that you're like, yeah, this is sick.

00:56:54.708 --> 00:56:57.244
- Yeah, we would receive
some really cool shit.

00:56:57.244 --> 00:56:59.370
- Lots of used video games

00:56:59.370 --> 00:57:01.193
and then I would just
take 'em all the GameStop

00:57:01.193 --> 00:57:04.193
and I'd be like, "Used video games."

00:57:04.193 --> 00:57:05.026
(Connor, Joey, and Garnt laughing)

00:57:05.026 --> 00:57:06.090
And then they'd be like, "$100 credit."

00:57:06.090 --> 00:57:08.427
I was like, "Two games I actually want."

00:57:09.570 --> 00:57:11.097
- That's so big brain, I
didn't think about that.

00:57:11.097 --> 00:57:16.097
- Not like the eight copies
of the Nintendogs ripoffs

00:57:16.530 --> 00:57:19.050
of like Puppiez and like all these weird,

00:57:19.050 --> 00:57:21.448
like Ponyz and Catz with a Z,

00:57:21.448 --> 00:57:23.780
so many weird games that I
would never be interested in,

00:57:23.780 --> 00:57:27.390
I was like, I can convert
these into games I wanna play.

00:57:27.390 --> 00:57:29.565
- Is it just people giving junk?

00:57:29.565 --> 00:57:30.930
Are they just trying to get rid of it?

00:57:30.930 --> 00:57:32.523
- Well we were given,

00:57:33.750 --> 00:57:37.110
a lot of stuff that we
were given were just like,

00:57:37.110 --> 00:57:39.060
people were looking for things to send.

00:57:39.060 --> 00:57:40.647
- Yeah, like I want to send you something.

00:57:40.647 --> 00:57:43.020
- And they didn't wanna
spend money on just sending,

00:57:43.020 --> 00:57:44.077
'cause they didn't even
know if we would see it.

00:57:44.077 --> 00:57:45.630
There was no guarantee,

00:57:45.630 --> 00:57:46.710
we didn't even know if we would see it.

00:57:46.710 --> 00:57:48.630
In fact, some of the things, I hope,

00:57:48.630 --> 00:57:49.980
it's probably better off we didn't see

00:57:49.980 --> 00:57:51.870
half of the things that we did see,

00:57:51.870 --> 00:57:53.280
but people would send us just old stuff

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:54.960
that they wanted to throw away.

00:57:54.960 --> 00:57:56.230
- Should've started a garage
sale or something, right?

00:57:56.230 --> 00:57:57.900
- Yeah, it was like old shirts

00:57:57.900 --> 00:58:00.390
that had not been washed in
years and had holes in them.

00:58:00.390 --> 00:58:02.377
- Yeah, because to them,
it was probably just like,

00:58:02.377 --> 00:58:04.140
"Yo, that's my shirt is the video!"

00:58:04.140 --> 00:58:05.766
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yes, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:58:05.766 --> 00:58:06.750
So then we had to start
being really selective

00:58:06.750 --> 00:58:08.040
about the things that we showed.

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:10.830
We had to stop showing the shit stuff,

00:58:10.830 --> 00:58:14.900
the dead stuff, the human body remains.

00:58:14.900 --> 00:58:16.590
(Joey laughing)
- The human bodies.

00:58:16.590 --> 00:58:17.850
- Anything that came from a human body

00:58:17.850 --> 00:58:19.320
was no longer making it in the videos

00:58:19.320 --> 00:58:20.790
up to a certain point.

00:58:20.790 --> 00:58:22.260
We realized that we were encouraging it.

00:58:22.260 --> 00:58:24.480
And then we out also
accidentally encouraged people

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:26.040
to send us money,

00:58:26.040 --> 00:58:29.460
because we had one
video where a few people

00:58:29.460 --> 00:58:30.570
sent us like $1 bills.

00:58:30.570 --> 00:58:32.370
So he's like, "Well we got 10 bucks!"

00:58:32.370 --> 00:58:33.900
And it was kind of a joke.

00:58:33.900 --> 00:58:36.750
And then we had to make a,
"Woo, we got 100 bucks!"

00:58:36.750 --> 00:58:37.583
And then it was like,

00:58:37.583 --> 00:58:39.600
we need to stop showing money altogether.

00:58:39.600 --> 00:58:41.460
And we realized that
anything that we showed

00:58:41.460 --> 00:58:45.300
was a direct call for people
to send us more of it.

00:58:45.300 --> 00:58:47.520
So eventually we had to
stop showing the weird stuff

00:58:47.520 --> 00:58:49.680
or the really grandiose stuff

00:58:49.680 --> 00:58:52.020
'cause we didn't feel
comfortable with either.

00:58:52.020 --> 00:58:54.000
- Yeah, someone might
feel bad if you show off

00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:55.357
something really amazing and they're like,

00:58:55.357 --> 00:58:57.930
"Oh, and I guess shout-out from Mexico,

00:58:57.930 --> 00:59:00.360
sent me this like shitty like keyring."

00:59:00.360 --> 00:59:02.996
- And a lot of people were
sending really sentimental

00:59:02.996 --> 00:59:06.360
handwritten things that
we could never actually,

