01:03:14.520 --> 01:03:16.230
and then it also had instructions

01:03:16.230 --> 01:03:17.670
to copy and paste for someone else.

01:03:17.670 --> 01:03:19.070
That was how I designed this

01:03:20.160 --> 01:03:22.770
copy-paste viral kind of thing.

01:03:22.770 --> 01:03:25.680
And I didn't know how well that was doing

01:03:25.680 --> 01:03:27.090
with the two lip sync videos,

01:03:27.090 --> 01:03:28.643
it's so weird to say that,

01:03:28.643 --> 01:03:33.510
that we did beforehand,
but I did get the bill,

01:03:33.510 --> 01:03:34.680
I did see the bill and it was

01:03:34.680 --> 01:03:36.540
a couple hundred bucks, you know?

01:03:36.540 --> 01:03:38.130
And I didn't know how to convert that

01:03:38.130 --> 01:03:39.330
into how many views this was getting,

01:03:39.330 --> 01:03:40.620
but I knew it was getting a lot of views

01:03:40.620 --> 01:03:41.460
or else I wouldn't have to pay

01:03:41.460 --> 01:03:44.073
a couple hundred dollars
for bandwidth on videos.

01:03:45.060 --> 01:03:48.210
So you actually used to
have to pay for bandwidth

01:03:48.210 --> 01:03:49.650
before YouTube offered the service.

01:03:49.650 --> 01:03:51.840
So we found out about
YouTube by me doing a search

01:03:51.840 --> 01:03:53.280
for one of the videos I found

01:03:53.280 --> 01:03:55.350
that someone else had
uploaded it to YouTube

01:03:55.350 --> 01:03:56.820
and I sent 'em a message.

01:03:56.820 --> 01:03:58.050
I was like, "Hey, this video,

01:03:58.050 --> 01:04:00.180
thank you so much for uploading it here.

01:04:00.180 --> 01:04:01.770
We wanna upload it ourselves.

01:04:01.770 --> 01:04:02.700
Do you mind removing it?"

01:04:02.700 --> 01:04:04.140
And they said, "Yeah, absolutely."

01:04:04.140 --> 01:04:06.870
So we created our
channel, Smosh was still,

01:04:06.870 --> 01:04:08.760
five letters, still available,

01:04:08.760 --> 01:04:11.130
and that was the beginning of that.

01:04:11.130 --> 01:04:12.840
- Damn.
- Damn.

01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:14.130
- Shoot, I forgot where we were going.

01:04:14.130 --> 01:04:16.317
We were going with why Defy was-

01:04:16.317 --> 01:04:18.275
- Why did Defy, like how
did that all come around?

01:04:18.275 --> 01:04:19.108
- How did they pitch it to you?

01:04:19.108 --> 01:04:19.941
- Yes, okay, okay.

01:04:19.941 --> 01:04:21.030
Yeah, okay, I got off on a side tangent

01:04:21.030 --> 01:04:22.809
'cause I was talking about
how I designed the website.

01:04:22.809 --> 01:04:24.810
- Trash Taste is all about
tangents, don't worry.

01:04:24.810 --> 01:04:26.536
- You're fitting right in, right in.

01:04:26.536 --> 01:04:27.873
- Yeah, oh yeah, seamless.

01:04:29.160 --> 01:04:33.750
So they came to us and they were like,

01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:35.100
we will take care of the website

01:04:35.100 --> 01:04:37.470
so you no longer have
that stress to deal with,

01:04:37.470 --> 01:04:39.360
'cause every week the website was crashing

01:04:39.360 --> 01:04:41.697
and I was figuring out how to keep it up.

01:04:41.697 --> 01:04:43.440
And they had this huge plan

01:04:43.440 --> 01:04:45.240
of all the things that
they were capable of

01:04:45.240 --> 01:04:47.130
and how they had the infrastructure

01:04:47.130 --> 01:04:49.200
to allow us to do all of our dreams.

01:04:49.200 --> 01:04:50.250
So they asked us,

01:04:50.250 --> 01:04:52.050
what do you want to do with this company?

01:04:52.050 --> 01:04:54.000
If you could make this company
anything, what would it be?

01:04:54.000 --> 01:04:55.470
And we had these huge lofty goals.

01:04:55.470 --> 01:04:57.600
They said, we got all of that on lock.

01:04:57.600 --> 01:04:59.910
We have the infrastructure,
we have the investors,

01:04:59.910 --> 01:05:01.290
we could make this happen

01:05:01.290 --> 01:05:04.110
and they offered us a certain
amount of money in stock.

01:05:04.110 --> 01:05:04.950
Sounded really good,

01:05:04.950 --> 01:05:08.610
more money than I thought
that I would ever see

01:05:08.610 --> 01:05:10.260
in my life before that.

01:05:10.260 --> 01:05:12.210
So I was like, that sounds great.

01:05:12.210 --> 01:05:14.430
Obviously this company's
eventually gonna go public,

01:05:14.430 --> 01:05:15.310
that's how they work, right?

01:05:15.310 --> 01:05:16.830
I did not know.

01:05:16.830 --> 01:05:19.140
My lawyer did not tell me that
that's not always the case.

01:05:19.140 --> 01:05:21.090
The people that we had surrounding us

01:05:21.090 --> 01:05:23.310
that were authority figures

01:05:23.310 --> 01:05:24.810
in some way to us on the situation

01:05:24.810 --> 01:05:26.930
did not break down the details

01:05:26.930 --> 01:05:28.770
of how that doesn't always happen

01:05:28.770 --> 01:05:31.140
and how that's actually a huge risk.

01:05:31.140 --> 01:05:32.363
- [Connor] Yeah, yeah, of course.

01:05:32.363 --> 01:05:34.050
- So they bought full ownership

01:05:34.050 --> 01:05:39.050
and they paid us in annual salaries.

01:05:39.300 --> 01:05:40.890
And they had this structure set up

01:05:40.890 --> 01:05:42.087
where it was like this year,

01:05:42.087 --> 01:05:43.620
eh, it's pretty shitty salary,

01:05:43.620 --> 01:05:45.930
but next year it's an okay salary.

01:05:45.930 --> 01:05:49.290
But by year four, you're
gonna love your salary.

01:05:49.290 --> 01:05:50.123
- Did that happen?

01:05:50.123 --> 01:05:52.197
- It did eventually play out that way

01:05:52.197 --> 01:05:54.673
and I was pretty comfortable with it.

01:05:54.673 --> 01:05:59.010
I was like, we are making
a good salary here,

01:05:59.010 --> 01:06:00.990
what do we really have to complain about?

01:06:00.990 --> 01:06:02.760
- And I guess they're
doing a lot of the stuff

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:03.900
that you used to have to do

01:06:03.900 --> 01:06:05.790
and they're looking after
things and managing it.

01:06:05.790 --> 01:06:07.410
But then also imagine as a YouTuber,

01:06:07.410 --> 01:06:08.790
it's probably quite difficult

01:06:08.790 --> 01:06:10.860
to give that control to someone else.

01:06:10.860 --> 01:06:12.930
- It was too, because did
give that control away

01:06:12.930 --> 01:06:14.970
and everything was not
how I would've done it.

01:06:14.970 --> 01:06:17.640
So I ended up working double time

01:06:17.640 --> 01:06:19.200
to teach them how to do the things,

01:06:19.200 --> 01:06:20.970
but then they were rehiring.

01:06:20.970 --> 01:06:22.590
And the way that bigger companies work

01:06:22.590 --> 01:06:23.970
is it's like you're never just building

01:06:23.970 --> 01:06:25.350
a one-on-one relationship with someone

01:06:25.350 --> 01:06:27.240
who you teach 'em once and they know

01:06:27.240 --> 01:06:28.920
and they keep going with that thing

01:06:28.920 --> 01:06:30.270
and then it's just a
thing that happens now.

01:06:30.270 --> 01:06:31.653
It's like, no, you're reteaching

01:06:31.653 --> 01:06:33.720
and reteaching and
reteaching until it's like,

01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:35.521
okay, let's abandon the website.

01:06:35.521 --> 01:06:37.740
And then it was like me
abandoning a lot of the things

01:06:37.740 --> 01:06:39.840
that I had a dream of growing

01:06:39.840 --> 01:06:42.120
because I was not able to keep that up

01:06:42.120 --> 01:06:44.310
and also do the videos.

01:06:44.310 --> 01:06:46.024
I had to figure out where to put my time.

01:06:46.024 --> 01:06:47.640
- Yeah, was there a specific point

01:06:47.640 --> 01:06:49.050
when you kind of realized,

01:06:49.050 --> 01:06:50.907
damn, this is not what I envisioned at all

01:06:50.907 --> 01:06:53.010
and I kind of want out?

01:06:53.010 --> 01:06:54.630
Was there a specific moment or was it just

01:06:54.630 --> 01:06:56.430
a buildup of a lot of things?

01:06:56.430 --> 01:07:00.660
- It was primarily a buildup
over a long period of time.

01:07:00.660 --> 01:07:03.910
It was about four years in when I realized

01:07:04.800 --> 01:07:06.780
that things just weren't looking right

01:07:06.780 --> 01:07:09.330
and I still had two years
on the contract left.

01:07:09.330 --> 01:07:12.570
And I think one particular thing was like,

01:07:12.570 --> 01:07:15.900
they hired someone that I did not know

01:07:15.900 --> 01:07:16.770
that they were gonna hire,

01:07:16.770 --> 01:07:17.910
and then all of a sudden I found out

01:07:17.910 --> 01:07:19.500
that they were now a full-time employee

01:07:19.500 --> 01:07:21.360
in this really important position

01:07:21.360 --> 01:07:22.687
that meant something to me and I was like,

01:07:22.687 --> 01:07:25.350
"Wait, I didn't realize that
was what was gonna happen."

01:07:25.350 --> 01:07:27.450
And also there were times,

01:07:27.450 --> 01:07:29.340
and I don't know how much I could say,

01:07:29.340 --> 01:07:31.126
but I'll see what I can do. (laughing)

01:07:31.126 --> 01:07:33.300
- Well, they don't exist anymore.

01:07:33.300 --> 01:07:34.323
- True, technically.

01:07:35.880 --> 01:07:40.547
There was a fundraiser that they put on.

01:07:40.547 --> 01:07:42.750
It was for a game,

01:07:42.750 --> 01:07:45.540
and they had just seen the
success of other people

01:07:45.540 --> 01:07:47.280
who had done fundraisers for games,

01:07:47.280 --> 01:07:50.040
Indiegogo, GoFundMe type things,

01:07:50.040 --> 01:07:54.360
and they raised like
$250,000 for these games,

01:07:54.360 --> 01:07:55.980
which were like mobile games

01:07:55.980 --> 01:07:56.970
but you could play it on the computer

01:07:56.970 --> 01:07:58.110
and it was like this really cool thing

01:07:58.110 --> 01:08:00.600
with all these inside
jokes from the community.

01:08:00.600 --> 01:08:03.540
And they said, "Oh, that's
cool, guys, you do that."

01:08:03.540 --> 01:08:04.567
And then we were like,

01:08:04.567 --> 01:08:06.090
"We don't have a concept for a game."

01:08:06.090 --> 01:08:07.590
They're like, "Ah, we'll
figure it out later.

01:08:07.590 --> 01:08:09.120
Just raise some money."

01:08:09.120 --> 01:08:10.942
- They just made the fundraiser

01:08:10.942 --> 01:08:13.110
and then they were afterwards
they were like, make the game?

01:08:13.110 --> 01:08:17.490
- And we said we need to have
something to pitch to people.

01:08:17.490 --> 01:08:19.467
And then they're like, "Cool,
just tell 'em the idea then."

01:08:19.467 --> 01:08:21.000
And then they're like, "You
come up with the idea."

01:08:21.000 --> 01:08:22.110
So I came up with the idea,

01:08:22.110 --> 01:08:24.750
Ian of course also, we
both came up with the idea.

01:08:24.750 --> 01:08:29.670
And they were like, "Cool, you
got the idea, let's do it."

01:08:29.670 --> 01:08:31.410
And I was like, "We need
to have concept art,

01:08:31.410 --> 01:08:33.750
we need to show a build of the game,

01:08:33.750 --> 01:08:34.770
we need to be able to show

01:08:34.770 --> 01:08:35.937
what we want to do with this thing!"

01:08:35.937 --> 01:08:37.890
And they're like, "Ah, we don't have time.

01:08:37.890 --> 01:08:39.240
Okay, we'll get concept art."

01:08:39.240 --> 01:08:42.390
So all we were able to do was
launch with this concept art.

01:08:42.390 --> 01:08:43.748
- Wow.
- Oh my God.

01:08:43.748 --> 01:08:47.640
- And launching a campaign, asking people,

01:08:47.640 --> 01:08:50.832
incentivizing your fans to give you money,

01:08:50.832 --> 01:08:52.050
it did not feel right to me,

01:08:52.050 --> 01:08:53.823
but I felt backed up into a corner.

01:08:56.010 --> 01:08:57.270
I think that's probably one of the things

01:08:57.270 --> 01:08:59.610
that I regret from my time there,

01:08:59.610 --> 01:09:02.730
is allowing myself to feel
backed up into a corner,

01:09:02.730 --> 01:09:04.890
to accept money from
people that were excited

01:09:04.890 --> 01:09:06.240
about this thing being built.

01:09:06.240 --> 01:09:08.160
Of course, we did accept money for that

01:09:08.160 --> 01:09:10.350
and I did put all of my heart and soul

01:09:10.350 --> 01:09:12.510
into making it as good
as it possibly could be.

01:09:12.510 --> 01:09:14.310
Still didn't live up to my expectations

01:09:14.310 --> 01:09:16.230
and I feel like if it was done
the right way it would've.

01:09:16.230 --> 01:09:19.230
But the moment that really stood out to me

01:09:19.230 --> 01:09:21.880
was there was some shadiness about

01:09:22.890 --> 01:09:24.000
obviously the way that they pushed

01:09:24.000 --> 01:09:26.640
to earn money for something
because they saw dollar signs.

01:09:26.640 --> 01:09:28.109
- Right, not because
it was a good project.

01:09:28.109 --> 01:09:30.900
- Rather than it being
like, those types of things,

01:09:30.900 --> 01:09:33.030
if you're gonna have
your audience fund them

01:09:33.030 --> 01:09:34.260
it should really be a passion project.

01:09:34.260 --> 01:09:35.850
- Yeah, absolutely.
- Exactly.

01:09:35.850 --> 01:09:37.350
- And I could have made a passion project

01:09:37.350 --> 01:09:38.790
'cause I really was excited about it,

01:09:38.790 --> 01:09:42.600
but it was so forced and rushed.

01:09:42.600 --> 01:09:47.600
And then there was some shady stuff

01:09:47.640 --> 01:09:48.540
about like the way

01:09:48.540 --> 01:09:51.810
that they contributed to certain donations

01:09:51.810 --> 01:09:55.110
to inflate the way that
certain things looked,

01:09:55.110 --> 01:09:56.400
and that's really all I'll say about that.

01:09:56.400 --> 01:09:57.840
But it made me feel so weird about it,

01:09:57.840 --> 01:10:00.810
I was like, I need out
as soon as possible.

01:10:00.810 --> 01:10:02.670
- I imagine when they were
doing stuff like that,

01:10:02.670 --> 01:10:05.340
the confidence you had in the company

01:10:05.340 --> 01:10:06.210
was probably a bit like,

01:10:06.210 --> 01:10:08.123
okay, if they're willing to do this stuff

01:10:08.123 --> 01:10:09.880
it must not be going so great.
- Yes.

01:10:09.880 --> 01:10:14.160
And I realized that the
way that they treated that

01:10:14.160 --> 01:10:16.380
was the same way that
they were treating me

01:10:16.380 --> 01:10:17.213
and all the other people.

01:10:17.213 --> 01:10:19.576
- Yeah, 'cause a lot of people
lost a lot of money, right?

01:10:19.576 --> 01:10:20.850
I mean, it was crazy.

01:10:20.850 --> 01:10:21.840
- What was it?

01:10:21.840 --> 01:10:22.890
- A lot of people lost a lot
of money, like YouTubers.

01:10:22.890 --> 01:10:24.480
- Oh yeah, yeah, eventually.

01:10:24.480 --> 01:10:27.270
Luckily, I wouldn't
say I foresaw anything,

01:10:27.270 --> 01:10:28.103
but it didn't feel right

01:10:28.103 --> 01:10:30.647
and I got out a year before
they ended up closing down.

01:10:31.710 --> 01:10:34.980
And another thing that
really was a turning point

01:10:34.980 --> 01:10:38.700
was I had made a Facebook page

01:10:38.700 --> 01:10:43.500
and it was, I don't know, I
had like 500, 600,000 followers

01:10:43.500 --> 01:10:45.630
or whatever you call
it on a Facebook page.

01:10:45.630 --> 01:10:48.103
- Subscriber, friends!
- Friends.

01:10:48.103 --> 01:10:49.353
- Friends, friends, yeah!

01:10:50.250 --> 01:10:53.130
And yeah, it was Smosh Anthony.

01:10:53.130 --> 01:10:55.680
And they were like, "Oh,
this has Smosh in the name

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:58.890
so we're gonna run this for you, okay?"

01:10:58.890 --> 01:11:01.020
I was like, "Okay, yeah, yeah, sure."

01:11:01.020 --> 01:11:03.540
And then I was like, I was kind of trying

01:11:03.540 --> 01:11:05.700
to start to silently transition out.

01:11:05.700 --> 01:11:07.230
I think they kind of picked up on the gist

01:11:07.230 --> 01:11:09.360
'cause we still were
contractually obligated.

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:12.690
But I was like, "Can I
get my Facebook back?"

01:11:12.690 --> 01:11:14.120
They're like, "No, it's ours."

01:11:14.120 --> 01:11:16.087
I was like,
- Oh.

01:11:16.087 --> 01:11:17.460
- "But I made that thing and you said

01:11:17.460 --> 01:11:18.960
that you just wanted to run it

01:11:18.960 --> 01:11:20.430
because you were gonna help
me post content on there."

01:11:20.430 --> 01:11:24.120
They're like, "It has Smosh
in the name, this is ours."

01:11:24.120 --> 01:11:26.382
- That's fucked up.
- Ooh.

01:11:26.382 --> 01:11:31.080
- I was like, "um, okay," and
then I easily settled on that.

01:11:31.080 --> 01:11:33.450
I was like, "Oh, okay, it's
fine, it's just Facebook.

01:11:33.450 --> 01:11:34.740
Who cares Facebook."

01:11:34.740 --> 01:11:37.747
And then they were like,

01:11:37.747 --> 01:11:41.657
"Hey, can we get access to
your Twitter and Instagram?"

01:11:41.657 --> 01:11:43.803
I was like, "no."
- No!

01:11:44.880 --> 01:11:47.370
And then I immediately changed the name

01:11:47.370 --> 01:11:49.310
from Smosh Anthony to Anthony Padilla.

01:11:49.310 --> 01:11:52.170
(all laughing)
- It's like, not on my watch!

01:11:52.170 --> 01:11:53.730
- After they asked me for access to that.

01:11:53.730 --> 01:11:55.620
- In a way it's kind of cool

01:11:55.620 --> 01:11:58.514
that they kind of forced
you into being like,

01:11:58.514 --> 01:11:59.977
you know what, fuck this,
I'm just gonna go on my own.

01:11:59.977 --> 01:12:02.150
I'm gonna fucking brand
myself, fuck these guys.

01:12:02.150 --> 01:12:04.410
- It really got me thinking like,

01:12:04.410 --> 01:12:06.050
could I do something on my own

01:12:06.050 --> 01:12:09.750
outside of this brand that I had created.

01:12:09.750 --> 01:12:12.483
And it was really sad because
I had made that brand.

01:12:13.390 --> 01:12:14.223
- Well, Smosh is yours, yeah.

01:12:14.223 --> 01:12:16.770
- When I was 14 years old I made that

01:12:16.770 --> 01:12:18.930
as a little shitty website as like a forum

01:12:18.930 --> 01:12:21.300
for me and my group of friends at school

01:12:21.300 --> 01:12:23.910
to go on and hang out with each other.

01:12:23.910 --> 01:12:25.170
It was really before social media,

01:12:25.170 --> 01:12:26.940
it was like, we were either talking on AIM

01:12:26.940 --> 01:12:29.190
or we were going on this website.

01:12:29.190 --> 01:12:32.910
And yeah, it was sad to
kind of let that part of me,

01:12:32.910 --> 01:12:36.544
like this baby that I had
brought into this world go.

01:12:36.544 --> 01:12:38.280
- God, it must be nerve-wracking

01:12:38.280 --> 01:12:39.900
trying to do something
yourself though, right?

01:12:39.900 --> 01:12:44.190
Because as a YouTuber,
ditching your channel

01:12:44.190 --> 01:12:46.110
which has millions and
millions of subscribers

01:12:46.110 --> 01:12:48.960
to start to start from
the ground up again,

01:12:48.960 --> 01:12:51.870
I think like you are one
of the first people I know,

01:12:51.870 --> 01:12:54.480
like first big creators,
to really branch off

01:12:54.480 --> 01:12:57.767
and try to do something
themselves with a new channel.

01:12:57.767 --> 01:12:58.796
- And do it well.
- And to succeed.

01:12:58.796 --> 01:13:01.352
- And there wasn't a precedent
set before that really

01:13:01.352 --> 01:13:04.500
that you could really succeed
by doing something new, right?

01:13:04.500 --> 01:13:06.690
- I mean, well, Fred did it.
- Oh yeah, that's true.

01:13:06.690 --> 01:13:09.510
- Fred had another channel
and then he did his thing.

01:13:09.510 --> 01:13:12.053
- Well, he did a few movies,
you did a few movies too.

01:13:12.053 --> 01:13:14.182
- I did a movie, I did one too.

01:13:14.182 --> 01:13:15.382
- How was doing a movie?

01:13:15.382 --> 01:13:16.440
- How was doing a movie?

01:13:16.440 --> 01:13:17.273
Oh man!

01:13:18.420 --> 01:13:20.430
That was also another weird experience.

01:13:20.430 --> 01:13:24.612
- But a part of me, 'cause
obviously, I mean, (laughing)

01:13:24.612 --> 01:13:25.588
it's not a good movie.

01:13:25.588 --> 01:13:27.502
(all laughing)

01:13:27.502 --> 01:13:28.398
I was just going, fuck it,

01:13:28.398 --> 01:13:29.666
I was trying to think of how to say it.

01:13:29.666 --> 01:13:31.121
It's not a good movie.

01:13:31.121 --> 01:13:33.060
- My heart, no, no!

01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:34.470
I put my heart and soul into that movie,

01:13:34.470 --> 01:13:35.870
it's the best movie!
- Wow, right in front of him?

01:13:35.870 --> 01:13:37.769
- Oh my God!

01:13:37.769 --> 01:13:38.733
This is fucked up!

01:13:40.650 --> 01:13:42.300
- It's a part of like, you-
- We invite him onto the show-

01:13:42.300 --> 01:13:45.450
- You invite me on here to talk
shit about my pride and joy?

01:13:45.450 --> 01:13:47.757
- Listen, I wanna talk to
you honestly about this.

01:13:47.757 --> 01:13:48.670
- Did you actually watch that?

01:13:48.670 --> 01:13:49.710
- Yeah, I loved it.

01:13:49.710 --> 01:13:50.610
- Dear Lord, wait.

01:13:50.610 --> 01:13:52.630
- I love it in a way of like-
- You said you loved it?

01:13:52.630 --> 01:13:54.153
- Yeah, it's a terrible movie.

01:13:55.155 --> 01:13:55.988
- It's so fucking bad.

01:13:55.988 --> 01:13:57.060
- It's so bad that it's good.

01:13:57.060 --> 01:14:00.720
- But it's that amazing
part of YouTube history

01:14:00.720 --> 01:14:02.850
where everyone had to have a movie.

01:14:02.850 --> 01:14:03.949
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:14:03.949 --> 01:14:05.919
- YouTube for some reason was
like, make a fucking movie.

01:14:05.919 --> 01:14:06.810
Pump them out.

01:14:06.810 --> 01:14:09.219
Fred had fucking John Cena in his movie.

01:14:09.219 --> 01:14:11.269
- Oh yeah, I forgot!
- Yeah.

01:14:11.269 --> 01:14:12.647
- I completely forgot about that!

01:14:12.647 --> 01:14:15.600
- It was just a fucking cool, weird time

01:14:15.600 --> 01:14:18.990
where people were making
movies whereas like,

01:14:18.990 --> 01:14:21.090
I mean, did you even want to make a movie?

01:14:22.530 --> 01:14:23.627
What was going on with that?

01:14:23.627 --> 01:14:25.260
'Cause I feel like there must have been

01:14:25.260 --> 01:14:26.670
so many people trying to put their things,

01:14:26.670 --> 01:14:28.500
trying to be like, "No, it should be this,

01:14:28.500 --> 01:14:29.333
it should be this!

01:14:29.333 --> 01:14:30.300
No, no, no, fuck this!"

01:14:30.300 --> 01:14:31.290
Oh, we should get John Cena in it.

01:14:31.290 --> 01:14:32.730
Everyone knows John Cena!"

01:14:32.730 --> 01:14:34.628
- Oh, we got Stone Cold
Steve Austin in ours.

01:14:34.628 --> 01:14:35.461
- Oh yeah.
- Well there you go.

01:14:35.461 --> 01:14:37.865
"We gotta get someone from the WWE!"

01:14:37.865 --> 01:14:40.440
- Well, I was like, John
Cena's not happening,

01:14:40.440 --> 01:14:42.600
it's gonna be Stone Cold Steve Austin.

01:14:42.600 --> 01:14:43.890
- Yeah, I wanna know about the movie.

01:14:43.890 --> 01:14:45.847
Like, what was all that?

01:14:45.847 --> 01:14:48.540
- Yeah.

01:14:48.540 --> 01:14:49.497
A lot of people had movies going on

01:14:49.497 --> 01:14:51.420
and a lot of people were
breathing down our neck saying,

01:14:51.420 --> 01:14:53.430
so when are you gonna make a movie?

01:14:53.430 --> 01:14:57.090
And we did kind of have this dream

01:14:57.090 --> 01:15:01.590
of eventually making the movie
version of a Smosh sketch.

01:15:01.590 --> 01:15:04.410
And we had a lot of, nothing concrete,

01:15:04.410 --> 01:15:06.420
but like, ooh, what
would the movie version

01:15:06.420 --> 01:15:07.320
of this look like?

01:15:08.310 --> 01:15:09.710
If we get a million dollars,

01:15:11.400 --> 01:15:12.630
we had some of the dumbest ideas

01:15:12.630 --> 01:15:17.160
of like where the movie
was all Smosh sketch budget

01:15:17.160 --> 01:15:20.040
and then we had millions
of dollars worth of money

01:15:20.040 --> 01:15:21.583
pumped into the final scene.

01:15:21.583 --> 01:15:23.604
(all laughing)

01:15:23.604 --> 01:15:25.200
That was our initial idea!

01:15:25.200 --> 01:15:27.630
- Because I remember on the Smosh channel,

01:15:27.630 --> 01:15:30.090
this was way before the
actual movie came out.

01:15:30.090 --> 01:15:32.310
You guys released an April Fool's video

01:15:32.310 --> 01:15:34.803
called Smosh The Movie.
- Yeah, we did.

01:15:34.803 --> 01:15:35.910
- Have you seen this video?

01:15:35.910 --> 01:15:36.900
- I think I've watched this.

01:15:36.900 --> 01:15:38.490
- This was like the first ever

01:15:38.490 --> 01:15:40.950
April Fool's YouTube video that I saw,

01:15:40.950 --> 01:15:42.690
but it was the first five minutes

01:15:42.690 --> 01:15:45.240
was like the most insane quality,

01:15:45.240 --> 01:15:47.640
like, oh my God, this looks like a movie!

01:15:47.640 --> 01:15:48.960
And then the remaining hour and a half

01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:52.180
was you and Ian walking
back home with no cuts.

01:15:52.180 --> 01:15:53.460
- [Anthony] Yeah, yeah!

01:15:53.460 --> 01:15:55.230
- And I thought, this is
the greatest thing ever!

01:15:55.230 --> 01:15:56.520
So then when I heard that you guys

01:15:56.520 --> 01:15:57.900
were actually making a movie,

01:15:57.900 --> 01:16:01.331
I was like, say syke, say syke.

01:16:01.331 --> 01:16:02.799
It's just another April Fools joke.

01:16:02.799 --> 01:16:04.710
- I will say, for YouTuber movies,

01:16:04.710 --> 01:16:07.117
what was the name of
the one with the ghost?

01:16:07.117 --> 01:16:08.287
- "Ghostmates"?

01:16:08.287 --> 01:16:10.950
- "Ghostmates," that was one
of the better YouTuber movies.

01:16:10.950 --> 01:16:12.480
I will say, that one was good.

01:16:12.480 --> 01:16:14.670
- That one we had a little
bit more freedom with

01:16:14.670 --> 01:16:16.260
and in a weird way though,

01:16:16.260 --> 01:16:18.180
we almost stripped out a lot of

01:16:18.180 --> 01:16:21.540
like what made Smosh
Smosh with that movie.

01:16:21.540 --> 01:16:26.540
So it felt weird, 'cause it
wasn't quite a good movie

01:16:26.970 --> 01:16:31.080
and then it wasn't quite
a bad YouTube movie,

01:16:31.080 --> 01:16:33.000
which is also entertaining
for its own reasons.

01:16:33.000 --> 01:16:33.837
- I think it's 'cause you and Ian

01:16:33.837 --> 01:16:36.750
are I think naturally very good on camera.

01:16:36.750 --> 01:16:39.150
Compared to how some
other people transitioned

01:16:39.150 --> 01:16:39.983
to the movies kind of thing,

01:16:39.983 --> 01:16:42.351
I think you and Ian did that a lot better.

01:16:42.351 --> 01:16:43.184
- Well, I think it definitely helped

01:16:43.184 --> 01:16:45.510
that you guys were already
acting in your sketches.

01:16:45.510 --> 01:16:46.726
- Yeah, there was a lot of that going on.

01:16:46.726 --> 01:16:47.997
- Yeah, acting.

01:16:47.997 --> 01:16:49.351
(all laughing)

01:16:49.351 --> 01:16:50.910
- Acting in your sketches.

01:16:50.910 --> 01:16:52.890
- I heard a lot about how I should

01:16:52.890 --> 01:16:55.140
improve my acting in the comments.

01:16:55.140 --> 01:16:56.400
- Really?
- Oh, yeah.

01:16:56.400 --> 01:16:57.690
- 'Cause they're gonna go into
it and they're gonna be like,

01:16:57.690 --> 01:16:59.640
you're a YouTuber, you can't act.

01:16:59.640 --> 01:17:01.387
so even if you did an amazing
job, people would be like-

01:17:01.387 --> 01:17:02.948
- Like stay in your lane.

01:17:02.948 --> 01:17:04.033
- Yeah, people would be like, no.

01:17:04.033 --> 01:17:05.697
- It would be like there's an expectation.

01:17:05.697 --> 01:17:07.590
But with the movies, with the first one,

01:17:07.590 --> 01:17:10.440
it was a lot of, you know, we
did have this dream to do it,

01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:13.320
but then other people, it was at Defy.

01:17:13.320 --> 01:17:16.470
They were like, "We
need to make this happen

01:17:16.470 --> 01:17:19.080
and we need to make it
happen in four months."

01:17:19.080 --> 01:17:20.490
- Four months!
- Four months?

01:17:20.490 --> 01:17:22.687
- Okay, I'm just throwing a random number.

01:17:22.687 --> 01:17:23.611
- Okay, I was about to say!

01:17:23.611 --> 01:17:25.082
- But it was something too little.

01:17:25.082 --> 01:17:25.979
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, not enough time.

01:17:25.979 --> 01:17:27.300
- It could have been six,
it could have been eight,

01:17:27.300 --> 01:17:29.940
but I think it was somewhere
probably around six months.

01:17:29.940 --> 01:17:30.773
- [Joey] Jesus.

01:17:30.773 --> 01:17:32.133
- And we were like,

01:17:32.970 --> 01:17:34.650
actually, no, I remember
it was after we got

01:17:34.650 --> 01:17:36.630
to a point in the scriptwriting process

01:17:36.630 --> 01:17:37.860
that they said this needs to happen

01:17:37.860 --> 01:17:39.570
in this very short amount of time.

01:17:39.570 --> 01:17:40.960
We're like, "We're not ready,

01:17:40.960 --> 01:17:43.860
we're still fleshing this thing out!"

01:17:43.860 --> 01:17:46.980
And it was a thing where we still wanted

01:17:46.980 --> 01:17:48.390
to make our videos on YouTube,

01:17:48.390 --> 01:17:49.290
we didn't want to give that up

01:17:49.290 --> 01:17:51.120
'cause we felt like we
would've had to dropped that

01:17:51.120 --> 01:17:54.780
in order to put all of our
effort into making a movie.

01:17:54.780 --> 01:17:57.090
And they were like, "Don't
worry, we can hire this person."

01:17:57.090 --> 01:17:59.610
And they gave us a few
options, they hired a person,

01:17:59.610 --> 01:18:02.638
the script was not anything
like I would've wanted it to be.

01:18:02.638 --> 01:18:03.510
It was a lot of like-

01:18:03.510 --> 01:18:04.676
- Just someone on Fiverr.

01:18:04.676 --> 01:18:05.640
(Joey and Garnt laughing)

01:18:05.640 --> 01:18:06.717
- Yeah.
- We found a guy.

01:18:06.717 --> 01:18:08.460
- Yeah, who is this guy?

01:18:08.460 --> 01:18:09.300
- It was a lot of like,

01:18:09.300 --> 01:18:13.080
here's what someone who
isn't in the YouTube space

01:18:13.080 --> 01:18:15.840
thinks our videos and thinks YouTube is.

01:18:15.840 --> 01:18:19.080
- Was it someone who like
worked in Hollywood movies?

01:18:19.080 --> 01:18:22.410
- It was like a Hollywood scriptwriter

01:18:22.410 --> 01:18:25.740
who did TV series and I
think some movies as well,

01:18:25.740 --> 01:18:27.600
and he did do some funny stuff,

01:18:27.600 --> 01:18:30.690
but it was very much like
an out touch perspective.

01:18:30.690 --> 01:18:33.240
And it was like, I mean,

01:18:33.240 --> 01:18:35.160
a lot of Smosh stuff was
cringey in its own right,

01:18:35.160 --> 01:18:39.647
but I was cringing at how out
of touch these things where.

01:18:39.647 --> 01:18:42.570
It was like, I could not do these things.

01:18:42.570 --> 01:18:46.710
There was this area where memes
go to die that was written

01:18:46.710 --> 01:18:48.960
where it was like a space.
- I remember.

01:18:48.960 --> 01:18:50.070
- Was that in the movie?

01:18:50.070 --> 01:18:52.191
- No, no, no, wait, fuck, no, was it?

01:18:52.191 --> 01:18:53.730
It does sound so familiar.

01:18:53.730 --> 01:18:55.290
- You know, I think that Ian
might have talked about it

01:18:55.290 --> 01:18:56.123
on a separate thing if
you've ever saw Ian.

01:18:56.123 --> 01:18:57.180
- Okay, that's probably why.

01:18:57.180 --> 01:18:58.200
This sounds so familiar.

01:18:58.200 --> 01:19:00.450
- Or maybe he and I both
talked about it at some point,

01:19:00.450 --> 01:19:01.770
but there was this part written

01:19:01.770 --> 01:19:03.570
where it was like where memes go to die.

01:19:03.570 --> 01:19:07.830
And he was like, "Tay Zonday's in there."

01:19:07.830 --> 01:19:09.180
And I was like, "Okay, first of all,

01:19:09.180 --> 01:19:10.560
you're trying to make us say

01:19:10.560 --> 01:19:12.292
that these people are dead memes."

01:19:12.292 --> 01:19:14.790
(Joey and Garnt laughing)

01:19:14.790 --> 01:19:15.840
That's kinda fucked up.

01:19:15.840 --> 01:19:19.890
And then also you are saying,
here's all these things

01:19:19.890 --> 01:19:22.410
that are not in the now,

01:19:22.410 --> 01:19:24.360
like it's just this weird period piece

01:19:24.360 --> 01:19:26.250
that just feels too soon
to be a period piece,

01:19:26.250 --> 01:19:27.303
you know what I mean?

01:19:27.303 --> 01:19:28.587
- Also it might age poorly.

01:19:28.587 --> 01:19:31.683
- It also just sounds like a
hello fellow children moment.

01:19:32.656 --> 01:19:35.292
It's like yes, I know
memes, I know meme culture.

01:19:35.292 --> 01:19:36.125
Well these that don't, right?

01:19:36.125 --> 01:19:37.606
- It was exactly like that.

01:19:37.606 --> 01:19:39.039
- Shillary Clinton, am I right?

01:19:39.039 --> 01:19:40.860
(all laughing)

01:19:40.860 --> 01:19:42.060
- It felt a lot like that.

01:19:42.060 --> 01:19:43.710
And then we did a lot of rewrites

01:19:43.710 --> 01:19:45.757
and it was still a very bad movie.

01:19:45.757 --> 01:19:48.142
(Joey and Anthony laughing)

01:19:48.142 --> 01:19:49.410
- Maybe that was a little too harsh.

01:19:49.410 --> 01:19:52.503
- How self-aware were
you on set to be like,

01:19:52.503 --> 01:19:54.558
guys, are we making a bad movie?

01:19:54.558 --> 01:19:55.890
- I think I was half self aware.

01:19:55.890 --> 01:19:57.450
I wasn't fully self aware.

01:19:57.450 --> 01:19:59.880
Part of me thought,

01:19:59.880 --> 01:20:02.217
like after a certain
scene happened I was like,

01:20:02.217 --> 01:20:04.200
oh, that might actually be good.

01:20:04.200 --> 01:20:06.330
Part of me did think it might
actually be a good movie

01:20:06.330 --> 01:20:07.830
depending on the way it was cut,

01:20:07.830 --> 01:20:10.830
depending on the way the
special effects looked.

01:20:10.830 --> 01:20:13.833
But the way that it came together
was very much, I was like,

01:20:14.970 --> 01:20:18.097
we were seeing like final
cuts of it, I was like,

01:20:18.097 --> 01:20:23.097
"Okay, well that's the movie."

01:20:24.150 --> 01:20:25.290
- Well, I mean, at least it's not as bad

01:20:25.290 --> 01:20:26.493
as Shane Dawson's movie.

01:20:27.810 --> 01:20:29.787
- I mean, it depends who you ask.

01:20:29.787 --> 01:20:31.560
- No, that one was really, really bad.

01:20:31.560 --> 01:20:32.730
- Was it?
- Have you seen it?

01:20:32.730 --> 01:20:36.480
- I did, but I saw it
like on the premier night

01:20:36.480 --> 01:20:38.490
where there was so much hype in the room

01:20:38.490 --> 01:20:39.690
from all the friends and family

01:20:39.690 --> 01:20:41.764
that it was hard to tell,

01:20:41.764 --> 01:20:43.470
is this a funny joke or am I just

01:20:43.470 --> 01:20:45.788
not in a good headspace right
now, you know what I mean?

01:20:45.788 --> 01:20:47.305
(all laughing)

01:20:47.305 --> 01:20:49.380
- You should go rewatch it sometime.

01:20:49.380 --> 01:20:50.670
- Maybe I will.

01:20:50.670 --> 01:20:52.530
- It's interesting, it's interesting.

01:20:52.530 --> 01:20:55.098
- I never even tried that.
- No, no, no.

01:20:55.098 --> 01:20:56.182
- It's not good.

01:20:56.182 --> 01:20:57.015
- Okay, okay.

01:20:57.015 --> 01:20:59.190
Well, it was a lot of fun though,

01:20:59.190 --> 01:21:03.510
and I think that some elements
from the Smosh sketches

01:21:03.510 --> 01:21:07.020
retained their Smoshiness.

01:21:07.020 --> 01:21:08.966
- Yeah, I think it's much
easier for me to sit here

01:21:08.966 --> 01:21:11.010
and be like, "You made a
fucking movie, you shit?"

01:21:11.010 --> 01:21:13.058
Like I could do any fucking better,

01:21:13.058 --> 01:21:14.947
like my movie would be good.

01:21:14.947 --> 01:21:17.423
"Oh, you gotta make it in six," what?

01:21:17.423 --> 01:21:18.840
I can't even spell.

01:21:18.840 --> 01:21:20.460
- It was a weird process,

01:21:20.460 --> 01:21:25.460
because we had trailers
and we had an acting coach

01:21:27.210 --> 01:21:30.210
and we had the pressure of like,

01:21:30.210 --> 01:21:32.160
this is the movie,

01:21:32.160 --> 01:21:34.170
it was something like a
million-dollar budget.

01:21:34.170 --> 01:21:35.003
So it was like-

01:21:35.003 --> 01:21:36.739
- You got like, the full
Hollywood experience.

01:21:36.739 --> 01:21:38.550
- It was the full Hollywood experience.

01:21:38.550 --> 01:21:41.190
And we had like a writer there on set

01:21:41.190 --> 01:21:43.500
to do rewrites in the moment if we needed

01:21:43.500 --> 01:21:45.150
to rewrite something for time,

01:21:45.150 --> 01:21:47.160
and there were a lot of scenes
that we had to cut for time

01:21:47.160 --> 01:21:48.690
and they were rewritten on them.

01:21:48.690 --> 01:21:51.720
It was such a different
experience where it was not,

01:21:51.720 --> 01:21:53.370
like I could still go to the director,

01:21:53.370 --> 01:21:56.070
who was Alex Winter and
he's amazing, you know?

01:21:56.070 --> 01:21:56.903
Is he the Bill?

01:21:56.903 --> 01:21:58.140
I forget if he's the Bill or Ted

01:21:58.140 --> 01:21:59.280
from "Bill and Ted's Excellent Adventure."

01:21:59.280 --> 01:22:01.110
The not Keanu Reeves, you know?

01:22:01.110 --> 01:22:02.984
- Oh, he's the Ted I think.

01:22:02.984 --> 01:22:04.650
- Ted, no, Bill, Bill.

01:22:04.650 --> 01:22:07.470
- I don't know, the one that's not Keanu.

01:22:07.470 --> 01:22:08.310
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:22:08.310 --> 01:22:09.690
- But Alex Winter's amazing

01:22:09.690 --> 01:22:12.530
and his kids were huge fans of Smosh

01:22:12.530 --> 01:22:15.443
so he really did want to give
it that genuine Smosh feel.

01:22:15.443 --> 01:22:17.266
- Dude, now I feel bad,
I just called it bad.

01:22:17.266 --> 01:22:18.680
(Joey, Anthony, and Garnt laughing)

01:22:18.680 --> 01:22:19.513
Put all this heart and passion

01:22:19.513 --> 01:22:21.090
and I just though it was bad.
- It was shit!

01:22:22.680 --> 01:22:24.480
- No matter how much heart and passion

01:22:24.480 --> 01:22:25.890
you put into something,

01:22:25.890 --> 01:22:28.293
if there are few elements
that are slightly off,

01:22:28.293 --> 01:22:31.680
it's gonna kind of all get
brought down to that level

01:22:31.680 --> 01:22:32.820
and I feel like that's what happened.

01:22:32.820 --> 01:22:35.550
There were a lot of great
things that could have been,

01:22:35.550 --> 01:22:37.830
but then it was like,
there were certain elements

01:22:37.830 --> 01:22:39.200
that just kind of brought it all down.

01:22:39.200 --> 01:22:40.034
- I mean, hearing,

01:22:40.034 --> 01:22:40.867
oh, we're gonna cut the
scene 'cause of time,

01:22:40.867 --> 01:22:42.450
that doesn't make you
probably feel very confident.

01:22:42.450 --> 01:22:44.010
You're like, that's not good.

01:22:44.010 --> 01:22:44.850
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:22:44.850 --> 01:22:46.320
But you know, a lot of times we're like,

01:22:46.320 --> 01:22:47.640
we're gonna cut this scene for time

01:22:47.640 --> 01:22:49.050
and we end up having this other scene

01:22:49.050 --> 01:22:50.420
that totally actually
works fine without it,

01:22:50.420 --> 01:22:52.593
it was like the rewrites were okay.

01:22:53.700 --> 01:22:55.440
- Were your sketches before scripted,

01:22:55.440 --> 01:22:58.440
so you already were
used to memorizing lines

01:22:58.440 --> 01:23:00.100
or were you mostly improvised before?

01:23:00.100 --> 01:23:02.940
- Yeah, yeah, they were
definitely scripted,

01:23:02.940 --> 01:23:04.530
but usually we would like
get a take that we like

01:23:04.530 --> 01:23:06.940
and then we'd be like, go
off-book for the final take,

01:23:06.940 --> 01:23:09.753
and half the time we'd go
with the off-book version.

01:23:10.680 --> 01:23:11.730
But it was a little bit different.

01:23:11.730 --> 01:23:14.380
So Smosh videos, we would shoot

01:23:15.300 --> 01:23:17.790
10 second clips of like, this thing.

01:23:17.790 --> 01:23:20.070
You say this thing, you say this thing.

01:23:20.070 --> 01:23:22.410
And when you do a movie,

01:23:22.410 --> 01:23:23.610
they really want you to get

01:23:23.610 --> 01:23:25.980
the full scene with this angle

01:23:25.980 --> 01:23:27.900
then the full scene with the next angle.

01:23:27.900 --> 01:23:29.680
- No jump cuts, no fucking jump cuts.

01:23:29.680 --> 01:23:31.860
- Yeah, yeah.
- Continuity.

01:23:31.860 --> 01:23:34.350
- So a lot of the acting in it

01:23:34.350 --> 01:23:36.270
was actually me trying
to remember my lines,

01:23:36.270 --> 01:23:39.120
and if I watch it, I'm just like,

01:23:39.120 --> 01:23:42.050
I'm just trying to
remember my lines right now

01:23:42.050 --> 01:23:44.160
for most of these lines.

01:23:44.160 --> 01:23:45.450
It didn't really get past that.

01:23:45.450 --> 01:23:48.630
And also I got in my
head a lot about like,

01:23:48.630 --> 01:23:49.920
I don't think this is gonna be the movie

01:23:49.920 --> 01:23:52.470
that I wished that could be.

01:23:52.470 --> 01:23:55.920
- It would be so hard to really
turn up and give it your all

01:23:55.920 --> 01:23:57.900
when it just doesn't feel
like it's going right.

01:23:57.900 --> 01:23:58.733
- It was hard.

01:23:58.733 --> 01:23:59.700
It was like, after the first day,

01:23:59.700 --> 01:24:00.930
even though there were so many people

01:24:00.930 --> 01:24:04.770
that had a lot of passion
and good intentions

01:24:04.770 --> 01:24:05.910
and a lot of people that did put

01:24:05.910 --> 01:24:07.380
their heart and soul into it,

01:24:07.380 --> 01:24:09.090
after the first day I was like,

01:24:09.090 --> 01:24:10.650
this doesn't feel like it's gonna be

01:24:10.650 --> 01:24:11.490
what I thought it would be,

01:24:11.490 --> 01:24:13.290
and then it was hard to get out of my head

01:24:13.290 --> 01:24:14.360
and me get out of that headspace.

01:24:14.360 --> 01:24:15.900
- And I guess you couldn't exactly

01:24:15.900 --> 01:24:19.500
go to Defy and be like,
"Hey, quick change."

01:24:19.500 --> 01:24:20.550
- Yeah, yeah.

01:24:20.550 --> 01:24:21.603
- It's not like a YouTube video-

01:24:21.603 --> 01:24:23.303
- Hey, we just shot the first day.

01:24:24.300 --> 01:24:25.680
- It's like, this sketch
kind of isn't working,

01:24:25.680 --> 01:24:27.710
let's scrap it and go to a new YouTuber.

01:24:27.710 --> 01:24:29.400
Let's do a new video, you know?

01:24:29.400 --> 01:24:30.233
- Yeah, yeah.

01:24:30.233 --> 01:24:31.920
Well, even down to the sketches,

01:24:31.920 --> 01:24:33.960
it was hard to say this video didn't work.

01:24:33.960 --> 01:24:34.793
- Oh really?

01:24:34.793 --> 01:24:36.540
- Because there was a lot of money

01:24:36.540 --> 01:24:38.460
that was invested into it.

01:24:38.460 --> 01:24:41.340
And we were given a
certain budget each month

01:24:41.340 --> 01:24:44.250
and if we scrapped it, it would be like,

01:24:44.250 --> 01:24:45.960
this is cutting into our overall budget,

01:24:45.960 --> 01:24:48.360
let's just try to make this work.

01:24:48.360 --> 01:24:50.640
So when that pressure starts building up

01:24:50.640 --> 01:24:53.910
and you have all these
other eyes and voices

01:24:53.910 --> 01:24:57.450
and budgets and restrictions,

01:24:57.450 --> 01:24:58.770
that's when I think things

01:24:58.770 --> 01:25:01.710
start to kind of go south
in those situations.

01:25:01.710 --> 01:25:02.790
And that's when I started to feel like

01:25:02.790 --> 01:25:05.100
things weren't really right.

01:25:05.100 --> 01:25:06.660
Even though like, I could totally see

01:25:06.660 --> 01:25:09.614
why you can't re-shoot a
sketch after you shoot it,

01:25:09.614 --> 01:25:11.940
obviously it makes sense.

01:25:11.940 --> 01:25:14.220
But I think if I was fully independent,

01:25:14.220 --> 01:25:15.053
there were a lot of things

01:25:15.053 --> 01:25:17.524
that I might have considered reshooting.

01:25:17.524 --> 01:25:20.790
But when we have this level

01:25:20.790 --> 01:25:22.980
where we want the
production value to be there

01:25:22.980 --> 01:25:24.480
and we kind of reached that point,

01:25:24.480 --> 01:25:25.410
it felt weird to go back.

01:25:25.410 --> 01:25:28.613
I now know that you could
go back and it's fine, but.

01:25:28.613 --> 01:25:30.060
- Back then you're like, holy shit!

01:25:30.060 --> 01:25:31.860
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Damn.

01:25:31.860 --> 01:25:33.450
- Would you ever want
to make another movie

01:25:33.450 --> 01:25:36.390
with the correct amount of
time and creative freedom?

01:25:36.390 --> 01:25:38.929
- Oh man.
- Anthony Padilla, The Movie.

01:25:38.929 --> 01:25:40.627
- (laughs) It's just me in it!
- This is you.

01:25:40.627 --> 01:25:42.720
- It's just him interviewing.

01:25:42.720 --> 01:25:44.280
- Oh man.

01:25:44.280 --> 01:25:47.370
I don't know, I don't
have any drive for that.

01:25:47.370 --> 01:25:49.410
I have been pushing myself more

01:25:49.410 --> 01:25:54.410
with the intros of my videos
on my channel that I do now.

01:25:54.790 --> 01:25:56.010
It is scripted.

01:25:56.010 --> 01:25:58.470
And I realize that a lot of the passion

01:25:58.470 --> 01:25:59.520
that goes into me writing the words

01:25:59.520 --> 01:26:01.875
makes it onto the camera when
I'm recording these intros.

01:26:01.875 --> 01:26:03.930
- Yeah, of course.
- So now I'm trying to,

01:26:03.930 --> 01:26:06.030
in a sense, maybe I'm
like reverse engineering

01:26:06.030 --> 01:26:07.320
a way for me to learn how to act,

01:26:07.320 --> 01:26:09.510
is by just actually giving the emotions

01:26:09.510 --> 01:26:10.343
when I write these things

01:26:10.343 --> 01:26:11.520
for me to actually give the emotions,

01:26:11.520 --> 01:26:13.440
I'm like, oh, when you say a line

01:26:13.440 --> 01:26:15.183
you're supposed to visualize it?

01:26:16.537 --> 01:26:17.850
Oh!
- You're not just saying it.

01:26:17.850 --> 01:26:19.592
- You don't just say the word?
- Yeah.

01:26:19.592 --> 01:26:21.810
- Oh, that's what acting is.

01:26:21.810 --> 01:26:23.220
So in a weird backward sense,

01:26:23.220 --> 01:26:25.320
I feel like I am pushing myself

01:26:25.320 --> 01:26:26.640
to learn how to act a little bit,

01:26:26.640 --> 01:26:30.193
and maybe, it depends, it
depends, but right now, no.

01:26:30.193 --> 01:26:33.690
- Because you've kind of done so much

01:26:33.690 --> 01:26:34.810
in the time that you've been on YouTube.

01:26:34.810 --> 01:26:37.380
Like, is there anything you haven't done

01:26:37.380 --> 01:26:40.320
that you want to do
eventually down the line?

01:26:40.320 --> 01:26:41.850
'Cause I feel like when
you just think about

01:26:41.850 --> 01:26:43.200
even just the Smosh stuff,

01:26:43.200 --> 01:26:45.900
it's like, you guys have
literally been at the top,

01:26:45.900 --> 01:26:47.961
you've done everything,
you've done all the big stuff.

01:26:47.961 --> 01:26:48.857
- Been at the top twice.

01:26:48.857 --> 01:26:50.310
- They've been at the top twice, dude.

01:26:50.310 --> 01:26:51.840
And you know, you've done the movies,

01:26:51.840 --> 01:26:54.483
you've done, did you release a book?

01:26:55.680 --> 01:26:58.080
- We released a graphic novel.

01:26:58.080 --> 01:26:58.913
- Okay.
- Ah.

01:26:58.913 --> 01:27:00.360
- Yeah, I had a lot of fun making it.

01:27:00.360 --> 01:27:01.590
Most people don't even know that existed

01:27:01.590 --> 01:27:02.640
'cause they did not market it.

01:27:02.640 --> 01:27:05.730
But I put my heart soul
into a graphic novel,

01:27:05.730 --> 01:27:07.080
but no actual book.

01:27:07.080 --> 01:27:08.420
So that's something I never have-

01:27:08.420 --> 01:27:09.600
- Is that something you'd wanna try?

01:27:09.600 --> 01:27:11.340
Or is there something
else where you just like,

01:27:11.340 --> 01:27:12.767
eventually I'd like to do it?

01:27:12.767 --> 01:27:13.830
- Maybe, maybe.

01:27:13.830 --> 01:27:15.360
I thought about putting together

01:27:15.360 --> 01:27:17.820
some kind of coffee table book

01:27:17.820 --> 01:27:21.120
that goes through the
different interviews I've done

01:27:21.120 --> 01:27:22.713
and talks about the different things

01:27:22.713 --> 01:27:24.510
that I learned from each interview,

01:27:24.510 --> 01:27:25.980
the things that I've taken from that

01:27:25.980 --> 01:27:28.050
and been able to apply to my own life,

01:27:28.050 --> 01:27:30.150
the moments that stood out to me

01:27:30.150 --> 01:27:34.810
as key things that I now
go back to and think about.

01:27:34.810 --> 01:27:36.523
- Yeah, so an autobiography.

01:27:36.523 --> 01:27:37.770
(all laughing)

01:27:37.770 --> 01:27:41.640
- A little bit, but hopefully
it could be also a way

01:27:41.640 --> 01:27:45.330
for people to learn through me learning.

01:27:45.330 --> 01:27:48.436
That's part of why I do
the interview series now.

01:27:48.436 --> 01:27:49.269
- I mean, to be fair,

01:27:49.269 --> 01:27:51.090
if there's anyone who has enough material

01:27:51.090 --> 01:27:54.113
to write a cohesive
autobiography, it's you.

01:27:54.113 --> 01:27:55.470
- I don't know.

01:27:55.470 --> 01:27:57.420
I feel like putting it in
the term of autobiography,

01:27:57.420 --> 01:27:58.383
I'm like, uh, no!

01:28:00.025 --> 01:28:03.222
- 'Cause it's a bit too self-important.

01:28:03.222 --> 01:28:05.486
It's like you say, I know so much more

01:28:05.486 --> 01:28:07.653
than all the other motherfuckers
who don't have books.

01:28:07.653 --> 01:28:08.486
- It's cause we've seen the YouTubers

01:28:08.486 --> 01:28:11.400
who have written
autobiographies, and you know-

01:28:11.400 --> 01:28:12.660
- And they're just like, come on.

01:28:12.660 --> 01:28:15.360
- I'm 24.
(all laughing)

01:28:15.360 --> 01:28:16.213
This is my life.

01:28:16.213 --> 01:28:18.243
- I struggled 12 times in my life,

01:28:20.068 --> 01:28:21.310
and I've recognized eight of them.

01:28:21.310 --> 01:28:22.440
- [Joey] Yeah, exactly.

