01:37:54.000 --> 01:37:56.130
and trying to balance
yourself is a lot easier

01:37:56.130 --> 01:37:58.080
than climbing up from
the bottom of the pyramid

01:37:58.080 --> 01:37:58.913
and trying to like stay relevant.

01:37:58.913 --> 01:38:00.570
- For sure, for sure.
- Yeah.

01:38:00.570 --> 01:38:04.380
- I think that some people have a goal

01:38:04.380 --> 01:38:06.360
of get to the top of this pyramid,

01:38:06.360 --> 01:38:08.070
just stay afloat up here.

01:38:08.070 --> 01:38:10.320
And of course, it is much more comfortable

01:38:10.320 --> 01:38:12.880
because you have the freedom to kind of

01:38:13.920 --> 01:38:17.640
apply more of these things that
you learn on a bigger scale.

01:38:17.640 --> 01:38:21.030
But at the same time,
there's so much more pressure

01:38:21.030 --> 01:38:24.030
and I think that that's
unforeseen to smaller creators.

01:38:24.030 --> 01:38:26.140
I had my period, I left
Smosh when I think we had

01:38:26.140 --> 01:38:29.010
22 or 23 million subscribers,

01:38:29.010 --> 01:38:31.410
huge amount of subscribers,
huge amount of pressure.

01:38:31.410 --> 01:38:33.779
I go off and do my own thing,

01:38:33.779 --> 01:38:35.760
channel starts with a couple million

01:38:35.760 --> 01:38:37.650
and they're people that don't
really know what to expect,

01:38:37.650 --> 01:38:39.270
I don't know what to give them.

01:38:39.270 --> 01:38:41.640
And there was still that
pressure there from me

01:38:41.640 --> 01:38:44.640
of how am I going to
entertain these people

01:38:44.640 --> 01:38:47.520
who expect me to be giving
the quality of content

01:38:47.520 --> 01:38:51.840
of a 22-million subscriber
channel but now it's just me,

01:38:51.840 --> 01:38:53.850
it's literally just me on my own.

01:38:53.850 --> 01:38:56.520
- I'm really curious about,

01:38:56.520 --> 01:38:57.930
obviously a lot of the viewers

01:38:57.930 --> 01:38:59.850
when you said, "Ah, I'm leaving Smosh,"

01:38:59.850 --> 01:39:02.100
I think a lot of people
would see that and think,

01:39:02.100 --> 01:39:04.470
oh, he's just leaving a channel.

01:39:04.470 --> 01:39:06.270
But I imagine there was a lot of

01:39:06.270 --> 01:39:08.730
probably behind the scenes or friendships

01:39:08.730 --> 01:39:10.173
that were strained by that.

01:39:11.040 --> 01:39:13.200
Like outside of just the
business perspective,

01:39:13.200 --> 01:39:15.180
did that put like a lot of
strain on your personal life,

01:39:15.180 --> 01:39:18.030
like leaving that and
having to kind of be like,

01:39:18.030 --> 01:39:19.680
all right, I'm leaving this big thing?

01:39:19.680 --> 01:39:20.817
'Cause you know, obviously Ian is there

01:39:20.817 --> 01:39:22.830
and a bunch of your friends worked there.

01:39:22.830 --> 01:39:24.420
- Well surprisingly, me leaving

01:39:24.420 --> 01:39:26.940
didn't put a strain in and of itself,

01:39:26.940 --> 01:39:29.430
like the idea of me leaving
and then me leaving,

01:39:29.430 --> 01:39:31.320
that didn't exactly put a strain.

01:39:31.320 --> 01:39:34.173
I think maybe afterwards
it did a little bit,

01:39:35.700 --> 01:39:37.740
really I think it was because audiences

01:39:37.740 --> 01:39:39.750
or viewers and commenters

01:39:39.750 --> 01:39:41.970
really were having all
these different things

01:39:41.970 --> 01:39:43.170
that they were saying about like,

01:39:43.170 --> 01:39:44.780
oh, this person's better
or this person's better,

01:39:44.780 --> 01:39:46.620
or you were better when you were here

01:39:46.620 --> 01:39:48.360
or they're better now that they're here,

01:39:48.360 --> 01:39:50.760
and I think that that
added a little bit of,

01:39:50.760 --> 01:39:52.107
it was unavoidable to see those.

01:39:52.107 --> 01:39:52.940
- [Connor] Oh, yeah, yeah.

01:39:52.940 --> 01:39:54.990
- I feel like you can't
turn off your brain

01:39:54.990 --> 01:39:57.660
from wanting to keep up with the comments

01:39:57.660 --> 01:39:59.457
even if you're not even
trying to look for them.

01:39:59.457 --> 01:40:00.900
I'm trying to see what people are thinking

01:40:00.900 --> 01:40:04.830
about this new thing that
I'm just trying to figure out

01:40:04.830 --> 01:40:06.450
and then I'm getting inundated

01:40:06.450 --> 01:40:08.056
with all these opinions from people.

01:40:08.056 --> 01:40:09.180
And I think that that does seep in

01:40:09.180 --> 01:40:10.530
when you're not ready for it.

01:40:10.530 --> 01:40:13.200
I do wish that I was more ready
for the types of comments-

01:40:13.200 --> 01:40:15.480
- How the fuck can you ready for that?

01:40:15.480 --> 01:40:17.490
- The only way to be ready for
it I think is to live in it.

01:40:17.490 --> 01:40:19.020
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

01:40:19.020 --> 01:40:21.390
But I also realized that
there is a bit of freedom

01:40:21.390 --> 01:40:23.220
in having a smaller audience.

01:40:23.220 --> 01:40:24.990
So it was a huge struggle,

01:40:24.990 --> 01:40:26.400
and my videos when I first left

01:40:26.400 --> 01:40:29.610
I think got a million views
each for a couple months,

01:40:29.610 --> 01:40:32.130
and I was like, "Oh, I
need to retain this."

01:40:32.130 --> 01:40:34.710
And then very quickly, once
I think people realized

01:40:34.710 --> 01:40:35.730
I didn't know what I was doing.

01:40:35.730 --> 01:40:38.370
I had no goal for the channel.

01:40:38.370 --> 01:40:40.530
I was like, "Each week we'll
figure out something new

01:40:40.530 --> 01:40:42.480
and I hope you watch it."

01:40:42.480 --> 01:40:47.480
And I quickly lost this
audience that I had

01:40:49.050 --> 01:40:52.380
and realized that they
were not there just for me.

01:40:52.380 --> 01:40:53.790
And it was a little bit
bit disappointing at first

01:40:53.790 --> 01:40:55.560
'cause I thought, oh I guess I have

01:40:55.560 --> 01:40:56.550
the freedom to do whatever,

01:40:56.550 --> 01:40:59.438
and I thought that I had that with me.

01:40:59.438 --> 01:41:00.960
And then it was a wake up call to realize,

01:41:00.960 --> 01:41:02.280
oh, but you have to make good content,

01:41:02.280 --> 01:41:05.460
you can't just expect them
to be there because it's you.

01:41:05.460 --> 01:41:06.783
Egotistical maniac.

01:41:10.920 --> 01:41:12.580
The views I think got to about

01:41:13.500 --> 01:41:16.530
like 30,000 to 60,000 views a video.

01:41:16.530 --> 01:41:17.719
- On the "Anthony Padilla" channel?

01:41:17.719 --> 01:41:18.737
- On the "Anthony Padilla" channel

01:41:18.737 --> 01:41:20.670
within the first year after.
- Really?

01:41:20.670 --> 01:41:23.940
- And at that time, if
videos weren't getting

01:41:23.940 --> 01:41:26.400
100,000 views or even more,

01:41:26.400 --> 01:41:28.140
people in the comments are just like,

01:41:28.140 --> 01:41:31.410
irrelevant, irrelevant, wow, fell off.

01:41:31.410 --> 01:41:33.974
- L + ratio!
- L + ratio + you fell off!

01:41:33.974 --> 01:41:36.150
(Garnt laughing)
- Yeah, yeah, a lot of that.

01:41:36.150 --> 01:41:38.190
- You basically had abandoned an audience

01:41:38.190 --> 01:41:39.150
for a different style,

01:41:39.150 --> 01:41:40.677
so then obviously that's gonna happen.

01:41:40.677 --> 01:41:41.880
But people don't see it like that.

01:41:41.880 --> 01:41:42.981
They're like, oh, your
name's worth so much.

01:41:42.981 --> 01:41:44.064
- They're like, what do you mean

01:41:44.064 --> 01:41:44.960
you're not doing sketches anymore?

01:41:44.960 --> 01:41:46.857
I thought you were gonna do
your own sketches now, right?

01:41:46.857 --> 01:41:49.260
- And I thought that I had
to, I thought I was beholden.

01:41:49.260 --> 01:41:51.570
And I think that the reason
that it got to that point

01:41:51.570 --> 01:41:54.120
is I felt like I was
trapped in this idea of

01:41:54.120 --> 01:41:57.120
I need to create sketches,
I need to be funny,

01:41:57.120 --> 01:42:00.960
I need to deliver something
that appeases that audience

01:42:00.960 --> 01:42:03.510
that I've learned how to appease.

01:42:03.510 --> 01:42:08.510
And I think getting down to,
I call them lower numbers,

01:42:09.360 --> 01:42:12.270
30 to 60k, I know a lot of
people are up and coming

01:42:12.270 --> 01:42:13.439
and that is a great number,
I don't wanna discount that.

01:42:13.439 --> 01:42:15.777
- I mean, perspective-wise, that's low.

01:42:15.777 --> 01:42:17.640
- But at the same time,

01:42:17.640 --> 01:42:21.510
I was kind of floundering
around at that number.

01:42:21.510 --> 01:42:22.920
And first of all,

01:42:22.920 --> 01:42:25.560
I had to get out of my head
from looking at the numbers.

01:42:25.560 --> 01:42:26.700
It wasn't about that anymore.

01:42:26.700 --> 01:42:29.190
I realized that I had an opportunity now

01:42:29.190 --> 01:42:32.790
to experiment without the
pressure of an audience.

01:42:32.790 --> 01:42:34.260
So that's when I started experimenting

01:42:34.260 --> 01:42:36.630
with all these different types of videos

01:42:36.630 --> 01:42:37.740
and all these different types of things

01:42:37.740 --> 01:42:40.897
and that's when I fell into doing

01:42:40.897 --> 01:42:43.167
"I Spent A Day With" videos,

01:42:43.167 --> 01:42:44.580
and it was without that pressure.

01:42:44.580 --> 01:42:47.250
And the first few did have that
pressure on me to be funny,

01:42:47.250 --> 01:42:49.477
so I was kind of, you know,

01:42:49.477 --> 01:42:51.270
"I spent a day with FLAT
EARTHERS" and it was kind like,

01:42:51.270 --> 01:42:53.250
look how ridiculous these people are.

01:42:53.250 --> 01:42:54.190
- Look how weird and wacky this is!

01:42:54.190 --> 01:42:56.160
- These people are weird!

01:42:56.160 --> 01:43:00.720
But slowly but surely over the next

01:43:00.720 --> 01:43:02.100
couple months or half a year,

01:43:02.100 --> 01:43:05.220
I started realizing that I
actually was really curious

01:43:05.220 --> 01:43:06.820
about different types of people.

01:43:08.953 --> 01:43:10.980
I did this video, "I
spent a day with FURRIES"

01:43:10.980 --> 01:43:15.000
and I had a company help me
reach out to these people,

01:43:15.000 --> 01:43:16.350
and I thought they were all local.

01:43:16.350 --> 01:43:17.183
When they showed up,

01:43:17.183 --> 01:43:19.560
I found out that one flew in
themselves on their own dime

01:43:19.560 --> 01:43:23.220
and then this person like
drove three hours to be there.

01:43:23.220 --> 01:43:25.680
And I was like, okay,

01:43:25.680 --> 01:43:27.520
and I already wasn't planning

01:43:28.770 --> 01:43:30.660
to say like, these people are weird,

01:43:30.660 --> 01:43:33.491
but a part of me was like,
I don't understand this,

01:43:33.491 --> 01:43:35.910
this is kind of a weird thing.

01:43:35.910 --> 01:43:39.063
But they get there and they're saying,

01:43:40.117 --> 01:43:43.805
"I used to watch Smosh all
the time, I love your stuff."

01:43:43.805 --> 01:43:44.876
- You're fucking weird!

01:43:44.876 --> 01:43:47.098
(all laughing)

01:43:47.098 --> 01:43:48.960
- So anyway, why are you here?

01:43:48.960 --> 01:43:51.990
- So I very quickly changed my perspective

01:43:51.990 --> 01:43:53.373
with the way that I went
into that interview.

01:43:53.373 --> 01:43:55.050
Like there was no way,

01:43:55.050 --> 01:44:00.050
and it felt weird to me doing
the flat-Earther episode,

01:44:00.390 --> 01:44:01.770
it felt weird to me,

01:44:01.770 --> 01:44:03.870
like I was going outside of my element

01:44:03.870 --> 01:44:08.870
to give the snarky commentary
channel perspective

01:44:09.390 --> 01:44:10.887
that was really popular
on YouTube at the time.

01:44:10.887 --> 01:44:12.870
I realized I was kind of falling into

01:44:12.870 --> 01:44:15.270
those tropes that were so popular.

01:44:15.270 --> 01:44:18.180
And I was like, I just feel
like I need to just be me

01:44:18.180 --> 01:44:19.590
and have a conversation with someone,

01:44:19.590 --> 01:44:22.530
be curious about them, try
to learn about their life.

01:44:22.530 --> 01:44:27.530
And in doing so, afterward
I had them reach out to me,

01:44:27.630 --> 01:44:30.030
they were really excited
about the way it turned out,

01:44:30.030 --> 01:44:32.177
but also a lot of commenters were like,

01:44:32.177 --> 01:44:35.160
"Whoa, this is the first
time I've ever seen

01:44:35.160 --> 01:44:36.300
furries represented in a way

01:44:36.300 --> 01:44:38.310
where they were treated with respect,

01:44:38.310 --> 01:44:40.350
and that is so weird, I've
never seen that before.

01:44:40.350 --> 01:44:43.300
I'm gonna send this to my
friends who don't understand me."

01:44:44.207 --> 01:44:45.540
And it was really then
that I realized like,

01:44:45.540 --> 01:44:47.793
oh, this is what actually feels right.

01:44:49.290 --> 01:44:52.890
I'm not appeasing an audience
I think I should be appeasing

01:44:52.890 --> 01:44:55.531
'cause it's the trendiness
of YouTube at the time

01:44:55.531 --> 01:44:57.630
or like this sense of humor

01:44:57.630 --> 01:44:59.730
that I felt like I needed to always be

01:44:59.730 --> 01:45:01.740
this character that I was on Smosh.

01:45:01.740 --> 01:45:04.320
I felt like I was able to
actually just be myself,

01:45:04.320 --> 01:45:06.540
and my editor then
helped me throw in jokes,

01:45:06.540 --> 01:45:07.680
but it wasn't up to me anymore,

01:45:07.680 --> 01:45:10.620
I was just making a
connection with the person.

01:45:10.620 --> 01:45:12.218
- Back to YouTube boomer talk.

01:45:12.218 --> 01:45:13.786
- (laughs) Let's go, let's go!

01:45:13.786 --> 01:45:15.990
- You talked about constantly learning,

01:45:15.990 --> 01:45:17.020
constantly seeing what's out there.

01:45:17.020 --> 01:45:19.380
There must have been a
point when you're just like,

01:45:19.380 --> 01:45:20.968
I am so out of touch right now.

01:45:20.968 --> 01:45:21.956
- Oh for sure!

01:45:21.956 --> 01:45:23.220
- 'Cause I constantly feel that.

01:45:23.220 --> 01:45:25.113
Sometimes I'm like, I get YouTube,

01:45:25.113 --> 01:45:27.660
I get the algorithm,
I get all the content,

01:45:27.660 --> 01:45:29.100
I get what people want to watch.

01:45:29.100 --> 01:45:30.720
Sometimes you just see
something and you're like,

01:45:30.720 --> 01:45:32.100
okay, I am a boomer.

01:45:32.100 --> 01:45:34.680
You see that one popular
video, and you're like, why?

01:45:34.680 --> 01:45:35.790
- Yeah.
- Why this?

01:45:35.790 --> 01:45:39.600
- There is this one
channel called Weegeepie.

01:45:39.600 --> 01:45:40.700
Have you heard of that?

01:45:40.700 --> 01:45:41.533
- No.
- No.

01:45:41.533 --> 01:45:42.410
- Okay.

01:45:42.410 --> 01:45:43.983
So I don't know how they do it.

01:45:46.946 --> 01:45:49.446
They have a 3D CG rig of Luigi

01:45:51.870 --> 01:45:55.230
and they animate him and
his face to react to things,

01:45:55.230 --> 01:45:57.330
or at least this is what the
channel was a few years ago,

01:45:57.330 --> 01:45:59.010
I don't know what they're doing now.

01:45:59.010 --> 01:46:02.460
So he was reacting to things
with Luigi sound effects,

01:46:02.460 --> 01:46:03.980
so like, "Waa, whoa!"

01:46:03.980 --> 01:46:08.470
And it was just like a
reaction channel by Luigi

01:46:09.450 --> 01:46:11.910
and some of them had millions of views

01:46:11.910 --> 01:46:15.166
and I was like, this is so bizarre.

01:46:15.166 --> 01:46:15.999
- That sounds incredible!

01:46:15.999 --> 01:46:18.630
- But I loved it.
- That sounds incredible!

01:46:18.630 --> 01:46:20.580
- I reached out, I
followed him on Twitter.

01:46:20.580 --> 01:46:22.920
I was like, "This shit, keep
it up, this shit's sick!"

01:46:22.920 --> 01:46:24.090
But it was so weird,

01:46:24.090 --> 01:46:25.680
and I was like, there is always

01:46:25.680 --> 01:46:28.530
something to learn about YouTube.

01:46:28.530 --> 01:46:30.333
There's always some weird corner.

01:46:31.440 --> 01:46:32.910
- I mean, now you're exploring those.

01:46:32.910 --> 01:46:33.743
- Yeah, now you're exploring, yeah.

01:46:33.743 --> 01:46:35.910
- Yeah, yeah, and those
are the types of people

01:46:35.910 --> 01:46:37.560
that I really, really enjoy talking to

01:46:37.560 --> 01:46:39.330
where it's this community
that's doing this thing

01:46:39.330 --> 01:46:42.573
that many people would
just think is bizarre.

01:46:43.500 --> 01:46:44.610
There's actually something to it

01:46:44.610 --> 01:46:45.810
and why does it catch on,

01:46:45.810 --> 01:46:47.670
and we could talk about
the emotional aspects

01:46:47.670 --> 01:46:50.280
of like the connection that people make

01:46:50.280 --> 01:46:51.900
and why do you think this is popular?

01:46:51.900 --> 01:46:53.190
- I think it's just really refreshing.

01:46:53.190 --> 01:46:54.960
I mean, obviously lately on the internet,

01:46:54.960 --> 01:46:57.540
and even when you started
it, it was very combative

01:46:57.540 --> 01:46:59.790
and everyone was always
at each other's throat,

01:46:59.790 --> 01:47:01.170
and I think it just came out at the time

01:47:01.170 --> 01:47:03.360
and kind of hit the
right note of just like,

01:47:03.360 --> 01:47:07.470
let's just talk, let's just
learn about each other.

01:47:07.470 --> 01:47:09.165
- Yeah.
- Just be open.

01:47:09.165 --> 01:47:11.670
- And I think it's, yeah, the success,

01:47:11.670 --> 01:47:15.270
it shows that the audiences
wanted that kind of content.

01:47:15.270 --> 01:47:18.330
People are curious about all
the types of different things,

01:47:18.330 --> 01:47:20.820
be it pony, pony?
- Pony players.

01:47:20.820 --> 01:47:23.959
- Pony players.
(Anthony laughing)

01:47:23.959 --> 01:47:26.780
- I wanna know them, I wanna know why.

01:47:26.780 --> 01:47:27.840
- In saying that though,

01:47:27.840 --> 01:47:30.030
there have been a couple of
videos I've seen from yours

01:47:30.030 --> 01:47:32.880
where you do kind of bring
back that old-school,

01:47:32.880 --> 01:47:35.850
a little bit cynical type
of interviewer type thing.

01:47:35.850 --> 01:47:37.410
The one about like you interviewing

01:47:37.410 --> 01:47:39.270
time travelers for instance,

01:47:39.270 --> 01:47:40.470
that one was like a little,

01:47:40.470 --> 01:47:42.750
I saw the little bit of
the cynical side of you,

01:47:42.750 --> 01:47:44.400
which was just as entertaining.

01:47:44.400 --> 01:47:45.690
- You have to be though.
- You have to be.

01:47:45.690 --> 01:47:47.970
- That was right after
the flat-Earthers episode,

01:47:47.970 --> 01:47:52.020
so I didn't quite detach
from that side of it.

01:47:52.020 --> 01:47:55.560
But I feel like now if I
were to approach that again,

01:47:55.560 --> 01:47:58.470
the topic that I'm like
a little bit like, hm,

01:47:58.470 --> 01:47:59.617
I think I would just outright say,

01:47:59.617 --> 01:48:02.460
"This seems completely unbelievable.

01:48:02.460 --> 01:48:03.930
Do you have people telling you that

01:48:03.930 --> 01:48:05.310
and how do you feel about that?"

01:48:05.310 --> 01:48:06.930
I feel like there's so many more

01:48:06.930 --> 01:48:09.510
interesting conversations
to have about that,

01:48:09.510 --> 01:48:11.880
and I think a lot of
people are either afraid

01:48:11.880 --> 01:48:14.280
to tell someone that their shit's whack

01:48:14.280 --> 01:48:17.880
or all they want to do is tell
them that their shit's whack

01:48:17.880 --> 01:48:20.008
and they don't wanna hear
why they do what they do.

01:48:20.008 --> 01:48:20.841
- Right.
- Right, right, right.

01:48:20.841 --> 01:48:23.010
- I think that if you
could just be transparent

01:48:23.010 --> 01:48:25.380
with anyone really,

