01:53:03.030 --> 01:53:04.530
the barrier for entry for YouTube now,

01:53:04.530 --> 01:53:06.900
I feel like even though YouTube is like,

01:53:06.900 --> 01:53:09.540
you can make anything on
YouTube and it'll be successful,

01:53:09.540 --> 01:53:12.540
still the quality of
content on YouTube now

01:53:12.540 --> 01:53:16.051
is so much higher than
it was 10, 15 years ago.

01:53:16.051 --> 01:53:17.460
- We're in like the Mr. Beast era

01:53:17.460 --> 01:53:20.566
where everything has to
be $100,000, I did this!

01:53:20.566 --> 01:53:23.036
I fucking stayed up for one week straight!

01:53:23.036 --> 01:53:25.427
It's like, all right, okay,
I can't compete with that

01:53:25.427 --> 01:53:27.150
'cause I just don't wanna do that!

01:53:27.150 --> 01:53:28.980
- Have you seen any of
Ryan Trahan's stuff?

01:53:28.980 --> 01:53:30.007
- I have, I have.
- I have, of course.

01:53:30.007 --> 01:53:32.644
- So I feel like he's been reinventing

01:53:32.644 --> 01:53:36.810
what YouTubers can now expect
to do with the platform,

01:53:36.810 --> 01:53:40.200
because he's taking some
elements of the Mr. Beast style

01:53:40.200 --> 01:53:44.580
with thumbnails that
look very nice and clean

01:53:44.580 --> 01:53:49.470
and he has an elevated
concept for this whole thing

01:53:49.470 --> 01:53:50.520
and it feels like a video game,

01:53:50.520 --> 01:53:51.353
it feels like you're watching

01:53:51.353 --> 01:53:53.033
this person's journey
unfold in front of you

01:53:53.033 --> 01:53:54.510
and you feel like you can't click away

01:53:54.510 --> 01:53:56.340
'cause you never know what's
gonna happen in any turn,

01:53:56.340 --> 01:53:58.080
but yet it is still a vlog.

01:53:58.080 --> 01:54:02.190
And he can still, because it is a vlog

01:54:02.190 --> 01:54:04.980
where the whole idea is
that it's low produced,

01:54:04.980 --> 01:54:06.360
he's able to make one a day.

01:54:06.360 --> 01:54:08.100
- It's quite nice just watching it,

01:54:08.100 --> 01:54:09.810
and you're like, this is nice.

01:54:09.810 --> 01:54:12.513
I just feel like it's not too crazy.

01:54:13.485 --> 01:54:14.640
- It's nostalgic.

01:54:14.640 --> 01:54:16.050
- I love watching a Mr. Beast video,

01:54:16.050 --> 01:54:18.367
but my God, I feel like
I'm a cog in a machine.

01:54:18.367 --> 01:54:20.550
- [Anthony] Yes, yes, yes, absolutely!

01:54:20.550 --> 01:54:21.742
- It's so much.

01:54:21.742 --> 01:54:24.390
- And there's so much streaming and text.

01:54:24.390 --> 01:54:28.080
It feels like a refined, modernized vlog

01:54:28.080 --> 01:54:31.920
that has yeah kind of
like an elevated gameplan.

01:54:31.920 --> 01:54:33.540
- The thumbnails are just the next level.

01:54:33.540 --> 01:54:34.373
- The thumbnails kill it.

01:54:34.373 --> 01:54:36.840
And I never would've thought
that a vlog thumbnail

01:54:36.840 --> 01:54:40.680
which is not clickbait,
'cause it's not clickbait,

01:54:40.680 --> 01:54:43.080
can look so good, right?
- Yeah.

01:54:43.080 --> 01:54:43.920
- I don't know who does it.

01:54:43.920 --> 01:54:46.980
- I remember when back when YouTuber vlogs

01:54:46.980 --> 01:54:51.450
were just you put on any
digital camera, handheld camera,

01:54:51.450 --> 01:54:54.540
and then Casey Neistat came
along and just used a DSLR

01:54:54.540 --> 01:54:58.167
and made basically like
mini short films every day.

01:54:58.167 --> 01:55:00.030
And people were like, what?

01:55:00.030 --> 01:55:01.890
You can do that, that's possible?

01:55:01.890 --> 01:55:04.868
And then everyone tried to
be Casey Neistat for a while.

01:55:04.868 --> 01:55:05.764
- And then he burnt out.

01:55:05.764 --> 01:55:06.750
- And then he burnt out.

01:55:06.750 --> 01:55:09.690
- I mean he was, every day.

01:55:09.690 --> 01:55:11.370
I mean, when he used to
talk about his schedule,

01:55:11.370 --> 01:55:12.930
he was like, "Yeah, I'd wake up at 4:00 AM

01:55:12.930 --> 01:55:14.220
and I'm editing it on the plane."

01:55:14.220 --> 01:55:15.840
I'm like, what the fuck!

01:55:15.840 --> 01:55:18.090
- But I think what we can
learn from that experience

01:55:18.090 --> 01:55:20.790
is that if you have something good,

01:55:20.790 --> 01:55:22.443
you don't need to overdo it.

01:55:23.310 --> 01:55:25.590
If you want it to have any longevity,

01:55:25.590 --> 01:55:27.900
part of the game plan
is to figure out a way

01:55:27.900 --> 01:55:30.900
for it not to take up your entire life.

01:55:30.900 --> 01:55:32.407
I interviewed Markiplier and he was like,

01:55:32.407 --> 01:55:35.100
"Burnout is when people
aren't refilling their bucket,

01:55:35.100 --> 01:55:36.450
they're constantly scooping

01:55:36.450 --> 01:55:38.220
out of their bucket for their content

01:55:38.220 --> 01:55:39.660
and they're not refilling it

01:55:39.660 --> 01:55:41.520
because they're not living
a life outside of that."

01:55:41.520 --> 01:55:44.010
You need to have that balance.

01:55:44.010 --> 01:55:45.560
- Have you ever been burnt out?

01:55:46.740 --> 01:55:48.485
- Yes, absolutely.

01:55:48.485 --> 01:55:49.318
(all laughing)

01:55:49.318 --> 01:55:51.511
- I would've been shocked if you said no.

01:55:51.511 --> 01:55:53.610
- I should've been like, never, never.

01:55:53.610 --> 01:55:56.010
Always a creative high.
- 17 years strong baby!

01:55:56.010 --> 01:55:57.270
- I was trying to be polite.

01:55:57.270 --> 01:55:59.256
The question should be,
so when did you burn out?

01:55:59.256 --> 01:56:01.110
(all laughing)

01:56:01.110 --> 01:56:03.780
- Honestly, you should have
just said it like that!

01:56:03.780 --> 01:56:04.860
Many different times,

01:56:04.860 --> 01:56:08.280
and every single time is
when I felt overworked.

01:56:08.280 --> 01:56:10.620
And then once it became
like part of a company,

01:56:10.620 --> 01:56:12.453
overworked, underappreciated.

01:56:13.530 --> 01:56:15.660
Felt like I wasn't being,

01:56:15.660 --> 01:56:20.660
like my opinions didn't quite
contribute to the creative.

01:56:21.300 --> 01:56:23.010
But then also outside of that,

01:56:23.010 --> 01:56:26.010
I was burnt out on my own outside of Smosh

01:56:26.010 --> 01:56:28.210
after I left and decided
to do my own thing.

01:56:29.100 --> 01:56:32.340
For a lot of that time, when
the views were dropping,

01:56:32.340 --> 01:56:33.660
I was also burnt out

01:56:33.660 --> 01:56:34.620
so it makes sense that people

01:56:34.620 --> 01:56:36.240
were not wanting to watch the content

01:56:36.240 --> 01:56:38.820
of someone who was burnt the
fuck out by their own content.

01:56:38.820 --> 01:56:40.650
That's like, that's not fun to watch.

01:56:40.650 --> 01:56:41.550
There's a feeling you can feel

01:56:41.550 --> 01:56:42.870
when someone is burnt
out by their content.

01:56:42.870 --> 01:56:45.270
- Yeah, and then when you
go to the the actual video

01:56:45.270 --> 01:56:47.397
and the top comment is like,
"He's run out of ideas."

01:56:47.397 --> 01:56:49.063
- Yeah, yeah, I'm like, "Fuck you dude!

01:56:49.063 --> 01:56:50.295
I got so many ideas."

01:56:50.295 --> 01:56:53.370
And I just sit there and
I'm like, "I have no ideas!

01:56:53.370 --> 01:56:54.220
They were right."

01:56:55.080 --> 01:56:56.010
- Call me out.

01:56:56.010 --> 01:57:01.010
- But you know, I think I
really started to reframe

01:57:01.590 --> 01:57:02.730
what was important to me

01:57:02.730 --> 01:57:04.740
and then I started not
taking it as seriously,

01:57:04.740 --> 01:57:06.330
not putting as much of my time into it,

01:57:06.330 --> 01:57:09.330
and I was able to live
a life outside of it

01:57:09.330 --> 01:57:11.940
and have thoughts that
were separate from my work,

01:57:11.940 --> 01:57:13.590
and I think it kind of helped

01:57:13.590 --> 01:57:15.164
reinvigorate to a certain degree.

01:57:15.164 --> 01:57:16.530
- Yeah, one thing I was gonna ask is like,

01:57:16.530 --> 01:57:20.760
because, compared to you
YouTube boomers right here,

01:57:20.760 --> 01:57:21.990
we are very much children.

01:57:21.990 --> 01:57:24.241
- Oh yeah, nine years,
seven years right there.

01:57:24.241 --> 01:57:25.074
- It's nine years, seven years.

01:57:25.074 --> 01:57:27.540
I just discovered what boogers taste like.

01:57:27.540 --> 01:57:30.870
So it's like, what I wanted
to ask you specifically

01:57:30.870 --> 01:57:34.740
is how much of longevity do you see

01:57:34.740 --> 01:57:37.350
as a YouTuber being a career?

01:57:37.350 --> 01:57:40.230
Because it's like, we
don't really have any,

01:57:40.230 --> 01:57:41.970
'cause like say with Hollywood

01:57:41.970 --> 01:57:44.700
or the acting industry or music industry,

01:57:44.700 --> 01:57:46.230
we already have examples of people

01:57:46.230 --> 01:57:49.617
who have had careers for like
30, 40, 50 years plus, right?

01:57:49.617 --> 01:57:51.690
But because YouTube is such a new thing,

01:57:51.690 --> 01:57:55.020
whereas I think the longest
is probably someone like you.

01:57:55.020 --> 01:57:57.060
It's like, do you see yourself doing this

01:57:57.060 --> 01:57:59.400
for the next 10, 15 years again?

01:57:59.400 --> 01:58:01.470
- I mean, it's already been half my life.

01:58:01.470 --> 01:58:05.700
So it feels like I can give my confidence

01:58:05.700 --> 01:58:09.300
to the idea that YouTube will
be around for a long time.

01:58:09.300 --> 01:58:12.660
They haven't made any glaring mistakes

01:58:12.660 --> 01:58:15.060
that are still glaring mistakes.

01:58:15.060 --> 01:58:17.400
It feels like they they've
made a ton of mistakes,

01:58:17.400 --> 01:58:19.230
absolutely, I'm not gonna
write them off completely.

01:58:19.230 --> 01:58:20.063
- Right, right, right.
- Yeah.

01:58:20.063 --> 01:58:21.750
- But it seems like over time

01:58:21.750 --> 01:58:24.060
they are remedying a lot of those,

01:58:24.060 --> 01:58:26.820
it feels like they actually
are caring about the community.

01:58:26.820 --> 01:58:28.020
There's plenty of issues,

01:58:28.020 --> 01:58:31.950
but it seems like enough people care

01:58:31.950 --> 01:58:35.820
to make changes to keep
the platform relevant.

01:58:35.820 --> 01:58:37.526
- 'Cause like, oh, go on, go on.

01:58:37.526 --> 01:58:39.863
- Oh, I completely lost my, say what.

01:58:39.863 --> 01:58:40.696
- I was gonna say,

01:58:40.696 --> 01:58:41.529
'cause obviously people are gonna be like,

01:58:41.529 --> 01:58:43.788
"What do you mean, they
did this fucking thing!"

01:58:43.788 --> 01:58:45.630
And it's like, I think,
as you were saying,

01:58:45.630 --> 01:58:48.930
as a whole platform, I think they meant,

01:58:48.930 --> 01:58:52.260
considering the amount of shit
that goes on the platform,

01:58:52.260 --> 01:58:55.650
they do a pretty good job
at keeping it relevant,

01:58:55.650 --> 01:58:58.650
pushing it forward, and
making it the place.

01:58:58.650 --> 01:59:00.057
Yeah, obviously there's places
where they fuck up massively.

01:59:00.057 --> 01:59:01.277
- Yeah, because I think definitely

01:59:01.277 --> 01:59:05.160
the one moment that YouTube
like did a fuckie-wuckie

01:59:05.160 --> 01:59:06.930
and everyone was like,
this is the end of YouTube

01:59:06.930 --> 01:59:09.020
was when demonetization
became a thing, right?

01:59:09.020 --> 01:59:10.530
And the adpocalypse came up.

01:59:10.530 --> 01:59:12.503
Everyone unanimously was like, this is it.

01:59:12.503 --> 01:59:14.940
This is where it dies.

01:59:14.940 --> 01:59:16.680
But it somehow managed
to stick around for what,

01:59:16.680 --> 01:59:17.513
five extra years?

01:59:17.513 --> 01:59:19.200
- That needed to happen though.

01:59:19.200 --> 01:59:21.090
Looking back at it, it
was an inevitability

01:59:21.090 --> 01:59:23.550
that it was gonna come to something like,

01:59:23.550 --> 01:59:25.500
a system like that was gonna
have to be put in place.

01:59:25.500 --> 01:59:27.900
- They implemented a lot of things

01:59:27.900 --> 01:59:29.190
that help with that though.

01:59:29.190 --> 01:59:31.080
Like on the backend, you
can upload a video now

01:59:31.080 --> 01:59:33.900
and it'll say, I'm not
sure if it's for everyone,

01:59:33.900 --> 01:59:36.276
I think it's for everyone,
please correct me if I'm wrong.

01:59:36.276 --> 01:59:37.109
I don't wanna be like-

01:59:37.109 --> 01:59:38.215
- You get YouTube privileges?

01:59:38.215 --> 01:59:39.631
- YouTube privilege right here!

01:59:39.631 --> 01:59:40.733
- I'm like, YouTube's great!

01:59:40.733 --> 01:59:43.170
And you're like, bro, we do
not have any of those features.

01:59:43.170 --> 01:59:44.880
- Well, let's find out.
- The 1%.

01:59:44.880 --> 01:59:47.550
- Okay, so what I will say
is when I upload a video,

01:59:47.550 --> 01:59:50.073
it says checking on the monetization,

01:59:50.970 --> 01:59:53.370
and then after a half hour,
45 minutes, an hour, whatever,

01:59:53.370 --> 01:59:55.500
it eventually does like the initial check

01:59:55.500 --> 01:59:57.360
and then you can have some confidence

01:59:57.360 --> 01:59:59.310
that it probably caught it
if it's gonna catch something

01:59:59.310 --> 02:00:01.050
